export const OPENERS_B = {
  "fish/killifish": [
    "Killifish in the aquarium trade are annual and non-annual egglayers, chiefly Nothobranchius, Aphyosemion, and Fundulopanchax. They are not the North American banded killifish, Fundulus, even when a shop uses the same common name for both. Adults span 1 to 4 inches, and the listed lifespan of 1 to 5 years is species-dependent because a fish from a drying pool and a fish from a permanent forest stream age on different clocks. Care is rated moderate to advanced for that reason. The animal is only as straightforward as the species that was actually in the bag.",
    "Many Nothobranchius live about a year because the wild pools they use disappear in the dry season. A heated aquarium does not cancel that calendar. Give the smaller annuals a tank in the 5-gallon part of the listed 5 to 20 gallon range, and move larger Fundulopanchax or a group toward the 20-gallon end. Temperature on the card is 68 to 80°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness 2 to 15 dGH. Those are envelopes, not one ideal number. A peat-swamp Aphyosemion and a soda-pool Nothobranchius should not be forced into the same bucket of parameters.",
    "Annuals are bred on peat. A shallow container of boiled, rinsed peat goes on the bottom, the pair drives eggs into it, and the moss is lifted out to be dried until it is damp rather than wet. Stored that way, the eggs wait out a resting period and are then wetted to hatch. That drying step is the usual breeding method for annuals. Leaving the peat submerged until it sours wastes the spawn. Non-annuals do not use this rest. Their eggs stay in water.",
    "Aphyosemion australe, the lyretail, is the longer-lived non-annual most keepers meet. It spawns on a yarn mop or fine plants, the eggs incubate wet, and the adults are kept for years rather than a single season. Treating a lyretail with a Nothobranchius drying routine dries out viable eggs. The reverse mistake, holding annual eggs underwater for months, is just as costly. Read the genus before you set up a spawning dish.",
    "The diet is carnivorous. Baby brine shrimp, daphnia, mosquito larvae, and similar frozen or live foods keep the belly rounded in a way flake rarely does. Temperament runs from peaceful to semi-aggressive, and males of one species will spar if the tank is a bare tray. Broken sight lines from leaf litter, oak leaves, and floating cover matter more than a bright display if several males share the water. Ammonia has to stay at 0 ppm, nitrite at 0 ppm, and nitrate under 20 ppm. These are small fish, and a dirty sponge filter shows up as clamped fins within a day.",
    "Buy a named species, decide whether you are keeping a peat-spawning annual or a mop-spawning non-annual, and size the tank to that fish. A mixed cup of unidentified killifish will either fight or fail to spawn, and an annual kept like a community tetra simply reaches the end of its year while the tank still looks new. The moderate-to-advanced rating is about that planning, not about exotic equipment.",
  ],

  "fish/koi": [
    "Koi are pond fish, ornamental carp in the lineage of Cyprinus rubrofuscus, and they do not belong in a 20-gallon aquarium. The card lists a minimum of 1000 gallons, or a pond, for fish that grow to 24 to 36 inches and live 25 to 35 years. A juvenile in a stock tank looks manageable for a season. The same fish a few years later is a heavy, long-lived animal that needs swimming length, depth, and filtration sized for carp waste, not for a living-room rectangle.",
    "The listed temperature span is unusually wide, 36 to 90°F, and the pH band is 7.0 to 8.5. That range describes a fish that can live outdoors through seasons, not a fish that should be cooked in a shallow tub in August or sealed under a solid lid of ice in January. Winter care is depth and an opening in the ice so gas can leave the water. The pond must not freeze solid. A circulating pump or a de-icer that keeps a hole open is part of keeping them, along with filtration that still runs when the fish are sluggish and feeding has stopped.",
    "Koi are peaceful omnivores and will eat almost anything offered, which is how they get fat and how the pond turns green. A staple of pellets made for koi, plus greens such as lettuce or duckweed in warm months, is enough. Bread, leftover rice, and constant handfuls of floating sticks raise ammonia. The only acceptable ammonia reading is 0 ppm, nitrite matches it at 0 ppm, and nitrate should stay under 20 ppm. In a 1000-gallon pond those numbers still move fast after a warm weekend of overfeeding.",
    "Filtration has to match the biomass of a 2-foot carp, not the gallon number on a box sold for goldfish bowls. A proper pond filter, settled mechanical media, and a biological stage that is not washed in chlorinated tap water are the minimum equipment story. Stock lightly at first. Several 8-inch koi in a brand-new 1000-gallon pond can outgrow the filter before the bacteria do. Intermediate care means watching that curve, not adding a fountain and walking away.",
    "They are social, outgoing fish and will learn the person who feeds them, but they are also jumpers when startled and they stir the bottom. A pond without a raised edge or a net over the shallow shelf loses fish in a thunderstorm. Sharp rock piles and herons take the rest. Plan the pond as a 25-year enclosure: shade for the high end of that 90°F ceiling, depth against a solid freeze, and a quarantine vessel so a new fish does not meet the herd on day one.",
    "People who want a patio tub of koi are looking at a different animal than the one on this card. If the water volume is not at the 1000-gallon pond minimum, choose a fish that stays small. Koi that are stunted in a tank do not become a manageable variety. They become crowded, short-lived carp with failing filters. The peaceful temperament does not make the body size optional.",
  ],

  "fish/kuhli-loach": [
    "Pangio kuhlii is the eel-shaped loach from Indonesia and Malaysia that spends the day under the gravel and comes out when the room goes dark. Adults reach 3 to 4 inches and, unlike many small bottom fish, can live 10 years or more. The group needs 20 gallons of sand, not a smaller box of caves. A smaller box can hold a juvenile for a month and then becomes a lidless trap of hiding spots with no floor left to forage.",
    "They are peaceful and shy, and a single kuhli is usually a fish you never see. Keep a group so that when one crosses the front glass, it is not an emergency dash between caves. Fine sand is the substrate that matches the way they burrow. Coarse gravel wedges the body and scrapes the barbels they use to find food. Leaf litter, narrow tubes, and a dimly lit corner do more for them than a pile of bright ornaments.",
    "Water on the card is warm and soft: 73 to 86°F, pH 5.5 to 7.0, and hardness only 0 to 5 dGH. That is not the hard tap water many community tanks run. If the source water is alkaline and rocky, cut it with reverse-osmosis water rather than dropping a bottle of acid in and hoping. Both ammonia and nitrite have to sit at 0 ppm in that soft water. Nitrate in a kuhli tank should remain under 20 ppm. They breathe through the gut as well as the gills when the water is low in oxygen, so a tank with a greasy surface film and no agitation is a poor home even if the numbers look acceptable on paper.",
    "The diet is an omnivore bottom-feeder menu. Tablets, frozen bloodworms, and the bits that reach the sand after the midwater fish have eaten will all be taken, but the loaches should not have to survive on leftovers alone. Feed after the lights go down so the food is still on the sand when they emerge. A tight lid matters because a kuhli can leave through a cable notch and dry on the floor. Care is moderate mostly because the fish is invisible until something is wrong, and a 10-year lifespan only happens if the sand stays clean enough to dig through.",
    "Tankmates should be calm fish that are not competing for the same caves at night. Fast, nippy barbs and large cichlids that dig up the sand undo the point of the group. Do not treat a kuhli as a scavenger that replaces maintenance. It will not eat fish waste, and a 4-inch loach still adds to the bioload of that 20-gallon minimum. Watch the group at feeding time once a week so a missing fish is noticed before the sand hides it for good.",
  ],

  "fish/molly": [
    "Mollies in the pet trade are Poecilia sphenops, P. latipinna, and P. velifera. The last two are the sailfins, and a mature sailfin male is the fish that pushes the size toward the top of the 3 to 5 inch range. A short-finned balloon or lyretail molly is the same genus with a different outline, not a smaller species that can live in a bowl. The minimum tank is 20 gallons, the lifespan is 3 to 5 years, and the temperament is peaceful until males spend all day displaying at each other in a tank that is too short.",
    "They are livebearers from Central and South America, and they breed constantly. A female can drop a brood about once a month and can store sperm, so a tank that started with four fish becomes a tank of fry without any further pairing. A 20-gallon aquarium can hold the adults. It cannot absorb unlimited generations. Remove fry to a separate grow-out or accept that the adults will eat some of them and the rest will stunt the water quality.",
    "The water on the card is hard and alkaline: pH 7.5 to 8.5, general hardness 12 to 25 dGH, temperature 72 to 82°F. Soft, acidic water that suits tetras leaves mollies shimmying and prone to wasting. They often do better with some aquarium salt as well as that mineral content, especially the sailfins. This is a husbandry preference for hard, slightly brackish conditions, not a medication schedule. Nitrate is tolerated higher than in many cards here, with a ceiling under 40 ppm, but ammonia and nitrite still have to read 0 ppm. Do not use the nitrate allowance as a reason to skip water changes.",
    "Care is rated easy, and the fish are omnivores that take flakes, spirulina, and frozen foods. The failures come from three practical mismatches: a soft-water community, a 10-gallon desktop tank, and no plan for the broods. Sailfin males need the full 20 gallons of length so the dorsal fin is not torn on rough plastic plants. Keep more females than males if you are not trying to breed, because one male in a small group harasses the females without pause.",
    "A molly that is clamping, flickering against the gravel, or losing weight in water below that pH band is not a mysterious disease by default. Check the hardness and the salt before you add anything else. Variatus platys and other hard-water livebearers can share the tank. Soft-water rasboras and wild bettas should not. If the goal is a single pretty fish in a nano cube, this is the wrong Poecilia.",
  ],

  "fish/mystery-snail": [
    "The common aquarium mystery snail is Pomacea bridgesii, a South American apple snail that stays about 2 to 3 inches. That is the pet species. Giant apple snails are a different problem: some of them are regulated because they damage crops, and they should not be ordered under a mystery-snail label or released anywhere. Plan on 5 gallons or more per snail, a lifespan of 1 to 3 years, and easy care as long as the water is hard enough to build a shell and the animal is actually fed.",
    "Breeding is easy and happens above the waterline. The female leaves the water and glues a pale clutch to the glass, the lid, or the filter housing. Those eggs need air. Submerging the clutch to stop it from hatching drowns the eggs, which is one way to limit numbers, but knocking it into the water and forgetting it only rots. If you want the babies, leave the clutch moist and above the surface until it darkens and the snails drop. A lid still has to keep the adults from climbing out while they spawn.",
    "The working pH on the card is 7.2 to 7.8, inside a broader listed range of 7.0 to 8.0. General hardness is 8 to 18 dGH and carbonate hardness 5 to 15 dKH. Temperature is 72 to 82°F. In soft, acidic water the shell pits and the snail fades even if algae is abundant. Offer a cuttlebone or a mineral block, and feed deliberately. An underfed mystery snail will eat live plants. A fed one still grazes soft leaves sometimes, but leftover wafers, blanched zucchini, and algae are the intended menu.",
    "The operculum is the trapdoor on the foot. A healthy snail can pull it shut and seal the shell. A snail that is hanging out of the shell, or that smells rotten when picked up, is dead and should come out before it pollutes the tank. A closed operculum and no movement can also mean the animal is resting, so give it a day in a small container of tank water before you discard it. Ammonia and nitrite stay at 0 ppm, and nitrate should remain under 20 ppm. A dead snail hidden in the plants will break those numbers overnight.",
    "They are useful algae grazers and they are not a cleanup crew that replaces water changes. Each 2-inch snail adds waste. Several of them in a 5-gallon tank, despite the per-snail gallon note being ignored, will foul the water. Copper medications sold for fish parasites kill apple snails. Move the snail out before any copper treatment, and do not return it until the copper is gone. The 1-to-3-year lifespan is normal. A snail that lived four months in a soft-water betta tank was not an old snail.",
  ],

  "fish/nerite-snail": [
    "Nerite snails sold for freshwater tanks belong to Neritina and Vittina. They stay small, about 0.5 to 1.5 inches, live 1 to 3 years, and are rated easy in a tank of 5 gallons or more. The fact people discover later is on the breeding line: they cannot breed in freshwater. Females still lay hard white eggs on wood, glass, and rocks. Those eggs do not hatch in a freshwater aquarium. Scraping them off is cosmetic. Waiting for a crop of baby nerites will not work, and it does not make these snails assassins.",
    "Assassin snails are a different family with a different job. A nerite is a grazer. It needs biofilm and algae on hard surfaces, plus calcium for the shell. In a brand-new tank that is still spotless, a nerite starves while the glass looks clean. Supplement with algae wafers or blanched greens if the tank is too tidy, and do not keep a crowd of them in a small aquarium that has already been wiped down to bare glass.",
    "The preferred pH on the card is 7.5 to 8.5, with a wider listed range of 7.0 to 8.5. Hardness should sit around 8 to 15 dGH and carbonate hardness around 5 to 12 dKH. Temperature is a bit cooler than many tropical cards, 72 to 78°F. Soft, hot, acidic water etches the shell and shortens that 1-to-3-year life. Ammonia at anything above 0 ppm, or nitrite above 0 ppm, is already too much. The tank they share should keep nitrate under 20 ppm.",
    "They are poor at righting themselves if they fall onto the sand and land aperture-up, and a nerite stuck on its back under a decoration can die in a closed shell. Place them on the glass or on rock when you add them. They also crawl out of the water and can dry on the rim if the lid is loose. Zebra, olive, and horned nerites differ in pattern more than in care. None of them will control a hydra outbreak or hunt other snails.",
    "Because the eggs never hatch in freshwater, the population you buy is the population you keep, minus natural deaths. That makes them a better choice than ramshorns when you want a set number of grazers. It also means a tank with no biofilm and a strict no-feed policy is a slow way to lose a 1-inch snail. Give them surfaces that are allowed to grow a film, mineral content in the ranges above, and no copper in the water.",
  ],

  "fish/oscar": [
    "Astronotus ocellatus is a large, messy South American cichlid, not a centerpiece for a community of tetras. The adult size is 12 to 14 inches. One oscar needs a minimum of 75 gallons. A pair needs 125 gallons or more. The lifespan is 10 to 20 years, so the tank you set up for a 3-inch juvenile is a long commitment, and the fish will outgrow every decoration you bought for the small body.",
    "They recognize the people who feed them and they will come to the glass. They also rearrange the aquascape. Slate caves, loose swords, and a carefully planted foreground become a pile by the end of the week. Use heavy rock seated on the glass, or accept a bare tank with a few unmovable pieces. Anything that fits in the mouth is food. Tankmates that seemed large enough at the shop are not safe once the oscar is a foot long. Keep them with other big, robust fish or keep them alone.",
    "Temperature is 74 to 81°F and pH is a wide 6.0 to 8.0. Hardness can run from 5 to 20 dGH. They are not fussy about a perfect blackwater number, and they are extremely fussy about waste. The diet is listed as carnivore and omnivore: pellets made for large cichlids, prawns, earthworms, and occasional greens. Feeder goldfish are a poor staple and bring parasites. Nitrate should stay under 40 ppm, and lower is better. Ammonia and nitrite at anything other than 0 ppm will burn the fins on a fish this messy, because one oscar produces more waste than a shoal of small community fish.",
    "Temperament is semi-aggressive and territorial. Two oscars that grew up together in 125 gallons can work. Two adults introduced in a 75-gallon tank usually cannot. Spawning makes them more violent, and they will defend a flat stone even if you had no plan to breed them. Care is rated moderate because the filtration and the water changes are the real work. A canister that was adequate at 6 inches is not adequate at 14. Rinse mechanical media often and change water on a schedule you can keep for a decade.",
    "Jumping is common when the lights snap on or a lid is left off during feeding. A hood that can take a hit from a heavy fish is part of the setup. So is a heater guarded from being shoved into the glass. The personality is the reason people keep oscars, and it does not shrink the minimum. If 75 gallons for one fish, or 125 for a pair, is not available, this is the wrong cichlid.",
  ],

  "fish/otocinclus": [
    "Otocinclus are very small suckermouth catfish, about 1 to 2 inches, with a lifespan of 3 to 5 years. Ten gallons is the smallest tank on the card, and only if it is already mature. They are peaceful herbivores, and the intermediate rating is not about aggression. It is about starvation. They need a mature tank with a real biofilm on the wood and the glass, and they need a group. A new aquarium that was set up the same week, wiped clean, and stocked with a lone oto is a common way to lose the fish in a fortnight.",
    "They are not Chinese algae eaters. The Chinese algae eater is a larger, more aggressive cyprinid with a different mouth and a different future. An oto stays tiny, does not eat black beard as a party trick, and will not police a dirty tank into balance. Soft green film and the aufwuchs on aged driftwood are the foods they are built for. If that film is gone, feed blanched zucchini, algae wafers, and repashy-style gels directly, and keep doing it. A thin oto with a sucked-in belly is already in trouble.",
    "Temperature is 72 to 79°F, a little cooler than a discus tank, and pH runs 6.0 to 7.5. Ammonia must be 0 ppm and nitrite 0 ppm. Do not let nitrate rise to 20 ppm with a group this sensitive. They are wild-caught more often than not, and a long trip plus a bare dealer tank is why so many die in the first month. Buy fish with round bellies, quarantine them in a mature holding tank that already has biofilm, and do not add them to a cycling aquarium.",
    "A group gives them the confidence to graze in the open. One oto hides, stops eating where you can see it, and fades. Half a dozen in a planted 10-gallon or, better, a larger matured tank, will work the leaves and the hardscape through the day. They do not need a strong current. They do need oxygen and stable water. A crash that a guppy shrugs off will wipe an oto group.",
    "Tankmates should be small and polite. Large playful barbs, hungry cichlids, and any fish that picks at a resting oto are a bad mix. Do not buy them as a solution for a hair-algae outbreak in a two-week-old aquarium. Age the tank, grow some film, then add the group. The 3-to-5-year lifespan is reachable when the first month is survived on purpose rather than by luck.",
  ],

  "fish/pacu": [
    "Pacu sold as vegetarians still become huge fish. The black pacu, Colossoma macropomum, and the red-bellied pacu, Piaractus brachypomus, are listed at 24 to 36 inches and 20 to 30 years. The minimum tank is 300 gallons. They are not small piranhas, even though the juvenile body and the shop story suggest a toothy oddball that will stay in a 55-gallon tank. A home aquarium that cannot hold a 2-foot fish is the wrong address, and some places restrict possession of pacu for that reason.",
    "The diet line says primarily herbivore. Fruit, vegetables, and plant matter are the staple, and nuts or seeds show up in wild diets. That does not mean the jaws stay harmless or the fish stays small. Teeth are molar-like and can crush, and a startled adult can injure a hand. Feed greens, pellets made for herbivorous giants, and the occasional prepared food. Do not build a diet of feeder fish and expect a vegetarian that tops out at a foot.",
    "Temperature is 76 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness 4 to 15 dGH. They are peaceful toward fish they cannot eat, which is a short list once they are adult. Nitrate should remain under 40 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are non-negotiable because the waste from a 2-foot herbivore is enormous. Care is rated expert because of the size, not because the water recipe is obscure. Filtration, water changes, and a tank that is actually 300 gallons or more are the expertise.",
    "Juveniles are round, silver, and often mislabeled. If you already have one in a tank that will never reach the minimum, rehoming to a public aquarium or a keeper with a pond-scale system is the honest move. Growth is fast on heavy feeding. Waiting to see whether this individual stays small is how 30-inch fish end up in 100-gallon tanks. Check local rules before you buy. A legal problem on top of a housing problem is a bad surprise at 2 feet.",
    "They are schooling fish when young and become more solitary as they bulk up, but the gallon floor does not drop for a single adult. A 300-gallon tank is the starting point on the card for an animal that may reach 36 inches and live for decades. Temperature spikes above 82°F in an undersized tub, and nitrate that sits well over 40 ppm, are how those decades get cut down. Plan the building, the floor load, and the filter before the fish.",
  ],

  "fish/peacock-bass": [
    "Peacock bass are Cichla, large predatory cichlids from Amazon drainages, and they are not community fish. Depending on the species, adults run 12 to 30 inches. The lifespan is 10 to 15 years. The minimum tank is 180 gallons. A butterfly peacock and a temensis do not share one adult size, so the species name matters before the tank is chosen, and the tank still has to sit at or above that 180-gallon floor.",
    "The diet is carnivore and piscivore. They eat fish. Tankmates that fit in the mouth will be eaten, and tankmates that do not fit may still be killed and ignored. This is a predator tank: one fish, or a carefully matched group in a system much larger than the minimum, with sturdy cover and no dither fish you are fond of. Temperature runs warm, 76 to 86°F. pH is 6.5 to 7.5 and hardness 5 to 15 dGH. A fish that eats whole meals should still see nitrate under 30 ppm. Ammonia remains 0 ppm after those meals, and nitrite remains 0 ppm with it.",
    "Care is advanced because of the combination of size, aggression, and food. A 180-gallon aquarium with a weak hang-on filter will not keep up with a fish that eats whole silversides and produces the corresponding waste. Use serious filtration and a water-change habit that holds nitrate under that 30 ppm line. Live feeder fish from a pet shop are a disease risk. Frozen fish foods, prawns, and prepared carnivore diets are the cleaner route, offered on a schedule that does not leave rot in the corner.",
    "They are patterned, fast, and impressive, and a juvenile looks like a fish that might share a tank with large tetras. It will not. By the time the body is past a foot long, the temperament on the card, predatory and aggressive, is the whole stocking plan. Lids must be secure. Cichla jump when they hunt a reflection or when the lights change. Decor has to be heavier than the fish, because a spooked adult will shove it.",
    "If the space you have is a living-room tank under 180 gallons, pick a different cichlid. Peacock bass do not stunt into a manageable pet. They stall, break down, and still try to eat the rest of the tank. The 10-to-15-year lifespan belongs to a fish that was given the length to sprint and the water changes to match its meals. Anything else is a holding pattern.",
  ],

  "fish/pearl-gourami": [
    "Trichopodus leerii is a larger gourami, not a dwarf that can be tucked into a nano tank. The adult size is 4 to 5 inches, the lifespan is 4 to 5 years, and the minimum tank is 30 gallons. A pearl gourami carries a filamentous pelvic feeler on each side, a thread the fish uses to test the space in front of it. Those threads tear on sharp plastic and get nipped off by the wrong neighbors, so the feelers are a husbandry detail, not just a field mark.",
    "Males build bubble nests. A mature male under floating plants will stack bubbles and try to spawn if a female is ready. The tank should allow a quiet corner at the surface and a gap of still air under the lid, because this is a labyrinth fish that takes breaths from above. A tight, agitated surface with no cover makes a spawning male abandon the nest and leaves the fish gulping in a current. Temperature on the card is 77 to 82°F and pH is a wide 6.0 to 8.0. A nesting male still needs ammonia at 0 ppm and nitrite at 0 ppm. Nitrate under the nest should stay under 20 ppm.",
    "They are more peaceful than three-spot gouramis, and the card rates them peaceful and beginner-level. That peace is conditional. Crowded pearl gouramis nip long fins. A 30-gallon tank with two males, a betta, and an angelfish is already crowded in the way that matters. One male with one or more females, or a single fish, in a planted 30 gallons is the stocking that matches the temperament line. Extra length helps more than extra height.",
    "The diet is omnivore. They take flakes at the surface, frozen bloodworms, and soft vegetable matter. Feed a mix so a fish that spends its life at the top still gets more than one color of flake. Care feels easy until a male is in nest-building mode and starts shoving tankmates out of the top corner. Have a second plan for that month, even if you are not raising the fry. The fry, if you let the nest stand, need infusoria and then fine foods, and the adults will eat them if the plants are sparse.",
    "Do not keep them in a cold room below that 77°F floor for long, and do not assume the peaceful label survives a tank of fin-nipping barbs. The threads, the bubble nest, and the 4-to-5-inch body are the three things that separate this fish from the smaller gouramis people confuse with it. A 30-gallon planted tank with a lid, gentle flow, and neighbors that leave long fins alone is the setup the card is describing.",
  ],

  "fish/pencilfish": [
    "Pencilfish are Nannostomus from Amazon drainages, slim fish of 1 to 2.5 inches that often hover on a slight diagonal in still water. A school in soft water lives about 3 to 5 years. The tank minimum is 10 gallons for a school of six or more. They are peaceful, and they are listed as easy to moderate because the school and the water are the actual requirements, not because the fish is aggressive.",
    "A lone pencilfish fades into a corner and stops showing the horizontal stripe that makes the group worth keeping. Six is the floor on the card, and a longer tank than a tall 10-gallon jar lets the school move. Soft cover from fine plants and a bit of tannin suits them. A bare, blasting community tank with large active barbs turns pencilfish into food or into fish that never leave the back corner.",
    "Water is soft and slightly acidic to neutral: pH 5.5 to 7.0, hardness 2 to 10 dGH, temperature 72 to 82°F. Ammonia at 0 ppm and nitrite at 0 ppm are the usual line, and nitrate should stay under 20 ppm. They are not the fish to cycle a tank with. Add them after the filter is already holding those zeros. A sudden alkaline buffer from a handful of crushed coral will sit outside the hardness range and dull the group.",
    "The diet line calls them omnivores and micro-predators. They pick at tiny foods in the water column. Crushed flake is a start. Baby brine shrimp, cyclops, and other small frozen foods are what put weight on a 2-inch fish that loses a race to bigger mouths. Feed where the school actually hovers, not only at the front glass where the gourami takes it all. Several species in the genus differ in stripe and in how strongly they tilt. The care envelope above covers the group sold as pencilfish.",
    "Temperament is peaceful and schooling, which means the danger is from tankmates, not from the pencilfish. Avoid fin-nippers and anything large enough to treat a 1-inch juvenile as a snack. A planted 10-gallon with a calm school can work. A 10-gallon that already holds a betta, a school of something else, and these fish does not. Give the six their own space or move the group into a larger tank that still respects the soft-water numbers.",
  ],

  "fish/peppermint-shrimp": [
    "Peppermint shrimp are small marine shrimp, about 2 inches, with a lifespan of 2 to 3 years. The card places them in a minimum of 10 gallons at beginner level, peaceful, and omnivorous. The water is reef water: 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm, ammonia 0 ppm, and nitrite 0 ppm. They are not a freshwater cleanup crew, and they will die quickly in a tank that is still mixing salt by guesswork.",
    "The shrimp people want under this name are Lysmata in the peppermint complex, often kept because they pick at Aiptasia. Not every red-striped shrimp in a shop cup is that animal. Lookalikes, including camel shrimp, are sold under the same common name and may ignore anemones or pick at coral polyps instead. Ask what was imported, and do not stock a reef on the assumption that any banded shrimp will clear a pest anemone and leave the corals alone.",
    "A 10-gallon tank can hold one of these shrimp if it is mature enough to offer film, detritus, and a frozen-food feeding. It is a tight home once fish are added. Peppermint shrimp are slow, nocturnal eaters and they get picked off by hawkfish, large wrasses, and anything else that hunts crustaceans. Give them rockwork with real gaps, feed mysis or a small pellet after the lights dim, and do not house them with the perching predators that treat a 2-inch shrimp as the meal.",
    "They molt. An empty shell on the sand is not a dead shrimp until you have counted the live animal. The new shell is soft, and that is when tankmates do the most damage. Iodine and trace elements in a normal reef mix, stable salinity inside 1.020 to 1.025, and no copper in the water are what get them through a molt. Copper treatments aimed at fish parasites are lethal to this shrimp. Move it out, or do not use copper in its tank at all.",
    "Peaceful does not mean they ignore every sessile animal. A hungry peppermint can sample polyps. Feed them so pest-eating is a bonus rather than the whole diet. Beginner care is fair only if the tank is already mixed, aged, and free of shrimp-eaters. A new 10-gallon with a swinging salinity and a damselfish is how a 2-to-3-year lifespan becomes a weekend.",
  ],

  "fish/pictus-catfish": [
    "Pimelodus pictus is a medium catfish that reaches about 5 inches and lives 8 to 10 years. Night-active 5-inch cats need 55 gallons of floor. That number surprises people who met the fish as a 2-inch juvenile in a dealer tank. They are nocturnal, they are schooling, and they carry sharp pectoral and dorsal spines that tangle in a net and puncture skin if you grab them. Intermediate care is about those spines, the school, and the mouth, not about an exotic pH trick alone.",
    "The card calls them peaceful omnivores. They are peaceful toward fish they cannot swallow. Anything small enough to fit in the mouth, including neon tetras and young shrimp, is food after dark. Stock them with fish that stay larger than that gape: bigger tetras, rainbowfish, peaceful cichlids of a similar build. Do not add them to a nano community and hope the nocturnal habit means they will not hunt.",
    "Water is warmer-cool and acidic compared with a rift-lake tank: 72 to 78°F and pH 5.8 to 6.8. That pH band is narrow. A tank buffered to 8.0 for livebearers is outside it. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm, nitrate under 20 ppm, and a soft sand or smooth gravel bottom protect the barbels. They forage all night. A rough pile of crushed coral will wear those whiskers down and the fish will stop finding food.",
    "Several pictus cats belong together. A single pictus spends the day wedged behind equipment and never shows the spotted pattern in the open. Several fish in 55 gallons use the length at night and rest together under driftwood by day. Feed sinking pellets, frozen bloodworms, and chopped prawn in the evening so the midwater fish do not take every bite. The spines mean you net them with a container or a fine mesh, not a coarse nylon bag that locks the spine and tears the fish.",
    "They are not upside-down catfish and they are not a pleco. They will not clean glass, they do not swim inverted as a lifestyle, and they will not stay in a 20-gallon tank for an 8-year lifespan. Plan the 55 gallons, the acidic water, and tankmates with enough body mass to stay off the menu. A pictus that is out in the day, gasping, is usually a water-quality problem. A pictus that vanishes until lights-out is behaving normally.",
  ],

  "fish/platy": [
    "Platys are Xiphophorus maculatus and X. variatus, small livebearers from Mexico through Honduras. Adults are 2 to 3 inches and live 3 to 5 years. A handful of adults can start in 10 gallons if you have a plan for fry. They are peaceful omnivores and are rated easy, which is true when the water is hard and when someone has a plan for the fry. Variatus platys tend to like it a little cooler within the listed range. Maculatus types are the short-bodied fish most shops mean by the plain name.",
    "Temperature is 70 to 78°F, pH 7.0 to 8.2, and hardness 10 to 25 dGH. Nitrate is allowed up to the under-40 ppm mark on this card, but ammonia and nitrite still have to be 0 ppm. Soft, acidic water that looks fine for a rasbora leaves platys listless. They do not need a marine mix. They do need minerals. If the tap water is very soft, crushed coral or a commercial mineral supplement aimed at livebearers belongs in the filter, with a test of GH so you land inside 10 to 25 rather than guessing.",
    "Females drop fry every few weeks, and they can do it from stored sperm after the male is gone. A 10-gallon tank with plants will hide some young and the adults will eat the rest. That is not a stable population plan. It is a slow overcrowding. If you want the babies, use a separate planted container or a breeder trap for the birth and then a grow-out. If you do not, keep fewer adults than you think a 10-gallon can hold, because the bioload is the adults plus whoever survives in the moss.",
    "They hybridize with swordtails. A mixed tank of platys and swordtails produces fry that are neither, and those fish muddy any later attempt to keep a line. If the strain matters, do not house the two Xiphophorus together. Males spar in a gentle way. Two males and one female in 10 gallons is still a lot of chasing. A planted footprint, more females than males, and ordinary flake plus some frozen food covers the diet.",
    "The easy rating fails in a hot, soft betta cube at 82°F with no minerals and a pile of fry the owner did not expect. Stay inside 70 to 78°F, keep the hardness up, and decide what happens to the next brood before it arrives. Mickey Mouse, sunset, and variatus tuxedo are color forms and closely related fish, not different care categories. The 2-to-3-inch adult is the size to stock for, not the inch-long juvenile in the shop cup.",
  ],

  "fish/pleco": [
    "The fish this page is about is the common pleco, Hypostomus plecostomus, from South America. Adults reach 12 to 24 inches and live 10 to 15 years. The minimum tank for an adult is 75 to 100 gallons. Many fish sold simply as plecos, with no species name, are this animal or something that gets just as large. A 3-inch juvenile in a 20-gallon tank is a temporary condition, not a small species.",
    "Do not buy one because someone said it will clean the glass. Commons rasp biofilm and wood, they eat more than algae, and they produce a great deal of waste while leaving plenty of algae behind. The diet is omnivore with a herbivore lean: algae wafers, driftwood to rasp, blanched greens, and some meaty foods. A pleco that is fed only what it can scrape from a bare tank starves slowly and still pollutes the water. Temperature is 72 to 82°F, pH 6.5 to 7.5, hardness 4 to 18 dGH. Once the fish is over a foot long, nitrate still needs to stay under 40 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm matter more as the fish passes a foot long and the filter has not been upgraded.",
    "They are peaceful and nocturnal. That peace does not include plants, which get uprooted, or small caves they outgrow and then get stuck in. Give an adult a long tank, real driftwood, and a cave it can leave. They are territorial toward other large loricariids. One common in 75 to 100 gallons is a reasonable reading of the minimum. Two adults need more room than the bottom of that range.",
    "Bristlenose plecos, Ancistrus, are the smaller animal people often meant to buy. Males grow fleshy tentacles on the head, and the fish stays a fraction of a common's length. If the tank is not in the 75-to-100-gallon adult range, a bristlenose is the honest substitute. Do not expect a common to stop growing because the tank is small. Growth slows, the body suffers, and the waste does not shrink in proportion to the hope.",
    "Care is moderate because of adult size and feeding, not because the fish is aggressive. A hood is still wise. Large plecos thrash when the lights come on and they can shove a heater. Handle them with wet hands and respect the odontodes, the small spines on the plates, which catch skin and nets. The 10-to-15-year lifespan is a housing decision made when the fish is still the size of a banana. Name the species at purchase, or assume you have a common and build the 75-to-100-gallon tank.",
  ],

  "fish/rabbit-snail": [
    "Rabbit snails are Tylomelania from the lakes of Sulawesi. The shell can reach 2 to 5 inches, which is a wide span because the genus includes several species sold under one common name. Lifespan is listed at 1 to 3 years, care is easy to moderate, and the minimum tank is 20 gallons. They are not a cold-water pond snail and they are not a nerite. The water they come from is warm and mineral-rich, and a soft acidic community tank is how the shell fails.",
    "Temperature on the card is 78 to 84°F, warmer than a standard tropical average. pH is 7.5 to 8.5, general hardness 8 to 15 dGH, and carbonate hardness 4 to 10 dKH. Sulawesi snails need ammonia held at 0 ppm and nitrite held at 0 ppm. Nitrate should remain under 20 ppm. If the room runs cool and the tank sits at 74°F for the sake of another fish, this snail is outside its range even when everyone else looks fine. Match the heater to the snail or do not keep it there.",
    "Breeding is slow. They are live bearers, and a female releases a single miniature snail rather than a clutch of eggs. You will not wake up to a hundred rabbits. You also cannot count on a replacement when an adult dies at the short end of that 1-to-3-year span. The baby needs the same hard, warm water as the adult, and it is easy to crush under a rock the parent can shrug off.",
    "They burrow. A sand bed deep enough for the shell to plow is the right substrate. Gravel chips the aperture and keeps them from moving the way they do in Sulawesi sediment. Feed them. Biofilm alone is not a reliable diet for a 4-inch Tylomelania. Algae wafers, blanched vegetables, and the occasional protein pellet keep them from wasting. An underfed rabbit snail rests, stops extending the long eyestalks, and is often mistaken for a shy healthy animal until the foot looks thin.",
    "They are peaceful toward fish and can be bullied by curious cichlids that nip the exposed foot. Stock them with calm fish in that 20-gallon minimum, preferably longer than tall, with a lid because a rabbit snail will climb. Calcium in the KH and GH ranges above is what keeps the shell smooth. A pitted shell in a 7.0 pH tank is a chemistry problem, not a mystery disease. Fix the minerals before you add another snail to replace the first.",
  ],

  "fish/rainbow-fish": [
    "Rainbowfish on this card are medium schooling fish, 4 to 6 inches, with a lifespan of 5 to 8 years. A school of fish this size needs at least 30 gallons. They are peaceful omnivores rated at beginner level, and the color people buy them for shows up in males as they mature, not in the silver juveniles lined up at the shop. Most of the fish sold under this name are Melanotaenia and the closely related New Guinea genera. The card does not split those species, so the numbers below are the shared envelope: 72 to 82°F, pH 6.5 to 8.0, nitrate under 20 ppm, ammonia 0 ppm, nitrite 0 ppm.",
    "A 30-gallon tank is the listed floor for fish that reach 6 inches and need a group. Use the length. A tall tank of that volume with a short footprint turns a school into a pile of fish treading at the glass. Several males with females, in a planted tank that still has an open lane, color up more reliably than a single male kept alone to be the centerpiece. They are not aggressive community fish, and they are too fast and too boisterous for a long-finned betta in a small box.",
    "Feed them as omnivores with a bias toward foods that support color. A quality flake, frozen mysis or brine shrimp, and some vegetable matter cover the diet line. They feed at the surface and in the middle, and they will outcompete shy bottom fish if you only sprinkle food at the top. Beginner care assumes the tank is already stable. A 4-inch rainbowfish is not a cycling tool, and nitrate that lives above 20 ppm fades the same colors the fish was bought for.",
    "Different species inside the hobby name get larger or smaller than the 4-to-6-inch band, so a fish identified only as a rainbow should be stocked as if it will reach the top of that range. Boesemani types are the usual large, late-coloring fish in this slot. They spend their first months looking plain. Selling them on or crowding them because the color never came is a timing problem. Give the school a year in clean water inside the pH and temperature bands before you decide the strain is dull.",
    "They jump when they are startled, especially in a sparsely planted tank with a gap at the back of the lid. Put a lid on before the first scare sends one over the rim. Keep the school together when you buy them so you are not adding one nervous fish to a tank that already has a hierarchy. The 5-to-8-year lifespan fits a group that was given 30 gallons or more of length, ordinary omnivore food, and nitrate held under 20 ppm. It does not fit a single juvenile in an uncycled desktop aquarium.",
  ],

  "fish/ram-cichlid": [
    "The ram on this page is Mikrogeophagus ramirezi unless a label says otherwise. German blue rams are a color line of the same species, not a harder fish. Bolivian rams are Mikrogeophagus altispinosus, a different animal with different needs, and they are not what this card is describing. Adult rams are small, 2 to 3 inches, and they live 3 to 4 years. A pair of rams needs 20 gallons of stable, aged water. Care is intermediate, the temperament is peaceful, and the diet is omnivore.",
    "The water is the part that kills them. Temperature is 78 to 85°F, warm on purpose, and pH is 5.0 to 7.0. They belong in soft, acidic, stable water, not in a freshly filled tank with a swinging pH and a dusting of ammonia. Rams die in new tanks. Age the aquarium, plant it, and confirm ammonia at 0 ppm and nitrite at 0 ppm for weeks before they arrive. Hold nitrate under 20 ppm or the short lifespan gets shorter. A water change that drops the temperature out of the 78 to 85°F band is as stressful as a missed change.",
    "Wild-caught rams and badly inbred domestic lines both arrive thin. Buy fish that are eating, with intact fins and a rounded belly, and keep them in their own 20-gallon tank or with calm tankmates that can handle the heat. A group of rams in a small tank becomes a series of territories once they pair. A pair in 20 gallons with cover is a better reading of the peaceful label than six adults on top of each other. They will guard a flat stone or a leaf when they spawn, and they are not peaceful toward other fish in that moment.",
    "Feed small omnivore foods: crushed pellets, frozen cyclops, baby brine shrimp, and a little vegetable matter. They are slow, deliberate eaters and lose food to faster tankmates. If you keep them with danios or barbs, the rams will starve in plain sight. Intermediate care is mostly this stability plus the heat. A heater that can hold 82°F in a cool room, a lid to keep that heat in, and a test kit you actually use are the equipment that matters.",
    "Do not treat a dying ram with a shelf of medications as the first response. Check the age of the tank, the temperature, and the nitrate. A ram in a two-week-old aquarium at 74°F is outside every important line on this card. The 3-to-4-year lifespan is short even when things go well. It gets much shorter in unstable, cool, or brand-new water. If you wanted the harder Bolivian species, this is not that page and these are not those numbers.",
  ],

  "fish/ramshorn-snail": [
    "Ramshorn snails in aquaria are Planorbella duryi and Planorbarius corneus, small lung snails from North America and Europe. The shell runs from a quarter inch up to about 1.5 inches. They live 1 to 3 years, care is rated very easy, and a tank of 5 gallons or more is enough for the snail even though the population may disagree. They breathe air at the surface as well as using the water, so a tank with a greasy lid and no gap can stress them in a way a fully aquatic snail might not show.",
    "Breeding is listed as very easy, and that is the whole reputation. They are prolific. A few snails become a carpet when there is leftover food. The honest control is feeding the fish less, not smashing snails every night. They eat scraps, soft algae, and decaying leaves, and they will browse tender plants if the tank is overstocked with them and underfed otherwise. A population explosion is a food audit. Nitrate on this card can sit under 40 ppm, higher than the limit for many fish here, but they still do better when it is lower. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm keep the water from killing the fish that share the tank.",
    "Temperature is 70 to 78°F. pH is 7.0 to 8.0. General hardness is 6 to 12 dGH and carbonate hardness 3 to 8 dKH. In very soft, acidic water the shell whitens and flakes. A piece of cuttlebone or a mineral source covers that without turning the tank into a limestone quarry. Pink, blue, and wild brown forms are the same kind of snail. Color is not a care category.",
    "They are useful scavengers and a poor choice if you want a planted tank with not a single extra snail. Eggs are laid in small gelatinous clusters, easy to miss on a leaf. If you need a set number of algae grazers and no babies, a nerite is the snail that does not hatch in freshwater. Ramshorns will. Quarantine plants if you do not want them, because eggs ride in on leaves more often than the adults do.",
    "A very easy rating still excludes copper. Any treatment that puts copper in the water will kill them, along with the shrimp you forgot about. It also excludes a sealed, boiling tank at the top of a discus setup. Stay inside 70 to 78°F and the pH band, feed the fish with some restraint, and the 1-to-3-year life of each snail is uneventful. The colony, if you overfeed, will look like a problem long before any one snail is old.",
  ],

  "fish/rasbora": [
    "The rasbora this card describes is the harlequin, a small peaceful schooling fish of about 2 inches with a lifespan of 5 to 8 years. Harlequins need 10 gallons for a small school, not for three fish. Care is beginner, the diet is omnivore, and the temperament is peaceful. The field mark is the black wedge on the rear half of the body, larger in males, which is why a loose school in a planted tank looks sharper than a single fish in a bare bowl.",
    "Trigonostigma heteromorpha is the usual scientific name behind that wedge. They come from soft, tannin-stained water in Southeast Asia, and the card's numbers match a gentle version of that: 72 to 81°F and pH 6.0 to 7.5. Ammonia and nitrite need to read 0 ppm. The wedge stays sharp when nitrate remains under 20 ppm. A 10-gallon tank works for a small school if it is planted and already cycled. It does not work as a starter aquarium that is still clouding in week one, and it does not work if the school is three fish.",
    "Keep enough of them that the group moves together. A harlequin that is alone clamps and loses the reason you bought the pattern. They spawn, in the wild and in a well-set tank, on the underside of broad leaves. You do not need to breed them to keep them, but a few Cryptocoryne or similar leaves give the school somewhere to hover. Flow should be gentle. A powerhead aimed through a 10-gallon tank exhausts a 2-inch fish.",
    "They are omnivores that take fine flake, micro pellets, and frozen baby brine or cyclops. Large floating pellets meant for cichlids are the wrong size. Feed a pinch they can finish. Bigger, faster tankmates will steal a slow feeding, so choose small peaceful neighbors: other small rasboras, peaceful tetras that can live at this pH, and calm bottom fish. Tiger barbs and restless danios turn the school into a group that hides in the plants.",
    "Beginner care here means stable water inside those ranges, a real school, and a lid, because even a small rasbora will jump through a feeding hole. The 5-to-8-year lifespan is realistic for a harlequin that was not cooked above 81°F and was not kept in a nitrate bath over 20 ppm. If the fish in the bag lack the wedge and the shop called them rasboras anyway, you may have a different species. Stock to the fish you can see, but do not ignore this card's 2-inch, 10-gallon, peaceful schooling frame when the fish is actually a harlequin.",
  ],

  "fish/red-claw-crab": [
    "Perisesarma bidens is a small coastal crab, not a fully aquatic fish-tank ornament. The carapace reaches about 2 to 2.5 inches, and the lifespan is only 2 to 2.5 years. Moderate is the rating because the animal is a climber that wants land and salt. The tank on the card is 10 gallons or more set up as a paludarium, with water that is strongly preferred brackish at a specific gravity of 1.005 to 1.015. A freshwater community tank with a deep water column and no land is the wrong build, even if a crab survives there for a few weeks.",
    "They need to leave the water. Provide rocks, wood, or a sloped bank that stays above the line, and keep the air humid. The card asks for humidity of 75 percent or higher above the water. A screen lid that dumps all the moisture into the room, with no cover over part of the land, dries the gill chamber. A tight lid matters for the opposite reason too: these crabs climb plumbing and will leave the tank. Temperature of the water is 72 to 82°F. pH is 7.5 to 8.5, general hardness 8 to 20 dGH, and carbonate hardness 6 to 16 dKH.",
    "Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are as important in a small brackish pool as in a reef, because the water volume in a paludarium is often much less than the tank's total gallons. Do not read 10 gallons of tank as 10 gallons of water if half the box is land. Size the filter, or the water-change habit, to the pool you actually poured. Breeding is listed as very difficult in captivity. Do not buy a pair expecting a colony. Males and females fight, and two males in a small paludarium will damage each other, especially around a molt when the shell is soft.",
    "They are opportunistic eaters. Pellets, blanched greens, and bits of frozen fish food are all taken, and so are slow fish and small tankmates. This is not a crab to drop into a tetra tank as a scavenger. Keep it with the understanding that anything it can catch is a possible meal, or keep it in a species setup. Molting crabs hide. An uneaten meal rotting in a 5-gallon pool of brackish water will spike ammonia past 0 ppm before you notice the crab has been under the wood for two days.",
    "Specific gravity should stay inside 1.005 to 1.015. Pure freshwater on one end, and full marine salt at reef strength on the other, are both outside that preference. Use a refractometer, not a guess from a handful of salt. Copper medications are lethal. The short 2-to-2.5-year lifespan means a crab that arrives as an adult may not have long, so the paludarium should be ready before the animal comes home. Land, humid air, brackish water, and a lid are the moderate part. The crab itself is hardy only inside that layout.",
  ],

  "fish/rosy-barb": [
    "Pethia conchonius is a South Asian barb that gets bigger than the tiny rose-colored fish many people picture. Adults are 4 to 6 inches and live 5 to 7 years. They need 30 gallons so the chasing has somewhere to go. They are active omnivores, rated easy, and the card calls them peaceful. They still nip fins. Long-finned tankmates in a short tank pay for that gap between the label and the mouth. A 30-gallon footprint with room to swim is what keeps the nipping from becoming the whole day.",
    "The temperature range is the other surprise: 64 to 77°F. This is a cool-water fish compared with a typical tropical community held in the low 80s. A tank at a steady 82°F sits above the listed ceiling. pH can run from 6.0 to 8.0 and hardness from 5 to 19 dGH, so they are less fussy about minerals than about heat and space. Keep nitrate under 30 ppm once those 4-to-6-inch bodies are grown. Ammonia still has to read 0 ppm, and nitrite does too. An unheated tank in a temperate room often lands inside 64 to 77°F more naturally than a heater set for discus.",
    "Males color into a deep rosy red when they are in good condition and have females to display to. Females stay plainer and fuller in the belly. A group, rather than a single male, is how that color shows up, and it also spreads the chasing. They are fast. Slow fish with trailing fins, especially in a crowded 30 gallons, get tattered. Choose tankmates that are similarly quick, or give the barbs enough length that they spend the energy on each other.",
    "Feed them as omnivores: flakes, small pellets, frozen foods, and greens. They will eat almost anything that hits the water, which makes them easy and makes overfeeding easy too. A 6-inch adult is a real bioload in the minimum tank. Change water often enough to hold nitrate under 30 ppm once the fish are grown, not only while they are 2-inch juveniles. Care feels easy because they eat and they do not hide. The work is the adult size and the cooler temperature.",
    "They spawn readily in cooler water with fine plants or a spawning mop, and a healthy female can produce a lot of eggs. You do not need to breed them to keep the color. You do need to avoid cooking them in a lidded tank aimed at 82°F year round. If the rest of the stock must live that warm, the rosy barb is the fish that should move, not the heater. The 5-to-7-year lifespan lines up with 30 gallons or more, 64 to 77°F, and neighbors that are not delicate fin-bearers.",
  ],

  "fish/rummy-nose-tetra": [
    "Rummy-nose tetras are small schooling characins, about 2 inches as adults, with a lifespan of 5 to 8 years. The tank minimum is 20 gallons. They are peaceful and the difficulty is intermediate. The common names and the science have shifted: fish sold as true rummy-nose are tied to Hemigrammus bleheri and to Petitella, and the red on the head is only worth buying if you keep a school. A pair in a 10-gallon tank never looks like the fish on the bag.",
    "The nose is a water-quality gauge. When conditions slip, the red recedes and the head turns pale long before the fish is belly-up. That is more useful than a vague promise that they are sensitive. Keep ammonia and the rest of the nitrogen cycle in a place where the red stays intact, and do not wait for a test kit if an entire school washes out at once. Nitrate creep, a dirty filter, and a neglected water change show up on the face. The card rates them intermediate because that signal is real, not because they are aggressive.",
    "Stock a proper school in the 20 gallons or more. Ten fish in a planted tank with open swimming room and a dark substrate show the red head and the black-and-white tail band. Three fish in a bright, empty tank go pale and hide. They are midwater swimmers. Leave the bottom to other animals and do not add them as dither fish for a cichlid that will eventually eat a 2-inch tetra. Peaceful neighbors of a similar size are the match.",
    "The purchase range on the card, about 5 to 100 dollars or more per fish, is wide because a true bleheri-type fish does not cost the same as a small false rummy-nose or a faded farm fish. Look at the red continuing toward the tail markings and at the body size. Setup costs on the same card run about 100 to 500 dollars or more, annual maintenance about 200 to 500 dollars, and food about 50 to 150 dollars a year. None of that spends well on a school that is too small to display or on a tank that lets the noses fade.",
    "They eat fine omnivore foods: small flakes, micro pellets, and frozen cyclops or baby brine. Feed the school where it swims so every fish gets a share. A 20-gallon tank is the floor for a group of 2-inch fish, not a suggestion to start in something smaller and upgrade if they live. Intermediate care is a cycled, planted tank, a real school, and the habit of reading the color of the head as part of the daily look. A pale nose in clean-looking water is a prompt to test, not a new strain.",
    "False rummy-nose fish and other red-headed tetras are sold beside them. They can be good fish and they are not this one. If the shop cannot tell bleheri or Petitella from the smaller lookalikes, buy on the pattern you can see and still give the school 20 gallons and clean water. The 5-to-8-year lifespan is available to a group that stays red because the water stays fit, not to a handful of pale fish in a crowded community that only gets a water change when someone remembers.",
  ],

  "fish/severum": [
    "Severums are large South American cichlids in the genus Heros. Adults reach 8 to 10 inches and live 8 to 10 years. An adult pair starts at 55 gallons, and that is before you add roommates. Temperament is semi-peaceful and the difficulty is intermediate. A juvenile with bars and a round body looks like a community fish. The adult is a deep, heavy cichlid that digs, eats plants, and wants a partner more than it wants a shoal of small tetras.",
    "They are often kept as a pair. A bonded pair in 55 gallons can work if the tank is arranged for them and not for a delicate aquascape. They will uproot stems, shove gravel, and defend a spawning site when they are ready. Semi-peaceful means they are not peacock bass and they are not harmless. Fast, deep-bodied tankmates that can take a shove are safer than angelfish with long fins or a school of small bottom dwellers. Many keepers simply give the pair the tank.",
    "The diet is the plant problem. Severums eat vegetation. A planted showcase becomes salad unless you feed enough greens that the leaves on the stems are less interesting, and even then some species of plant will not survive. Blanched spinach, peas, spirulina pellets, and a cichlid staple with some meaty frozen food match an omnivore that leans green. Feed on a schedule. An underfed severum does not become a polite algae eater. It strips the tank and then gets thin anyway.",
    "The card lists a setup in the broad band of about 100 to 500 dollars or more, fish around 5 to 100 dollars or more each, annual maintenance around 200 to 500 dollars, and food around 50 to 150 dollars a year. The 55-gallon minimum is where that setup money should go. A cheaper 30-gallon tank saves cash and fails the adult size. Intermediate care is filtration and water changes sized for an 8-to-10-inch cichlid that digs in the mulm, plus the judgment to separate a pair that is beating each other up instead of bonding.",
    "They recognize a routine and they will beg at the glass, which makes overfeeding easy. A thick fish in a 55-gallon tank with shredded plants and a neglected filter is the usual failure, not a mysterious incompatibility. Give them length, heavy decor they cannot stack against the heater, and food that includes plants you intended to sacrifice. The 8-to-10-year lifespan fits that kind of tank. It does not fit a juvenile left in a community until the day it swallows a tetra and uproots the last sword.",
    "Name the fish as a severum when you buy it, and assume it will reach the top of the 8-to-10-inch range. Gold, green, and turquoise forms are still Heros and still need 55 gallons. A pair needs more than the minimum if they are sharing the tank with other large cichlids. Semi-peaceful is a description of a calm pair with space, not a promise that three adults and a school of small fish will sort themselves out.",
  ],

  "fish/siamese-algae-eater": [
    "The Siamese algae eater is the cyprinid that actually eats black beard algae, and the adult size on this card is 6 inches with a lifespan of about 10 years. A fish that reaches 6 inches needs 20 gallons or more. Temperament is listed as peaceful and the difficulty as beginner. Those words describe Crossocheilus oblongus and Crossocheilus langei, not every slim fish with a black stripe that a shop has labeled algae eater.",
    "Flying foxes and Chinese algae eaters are the look-alikes, and they are not this fish. A Chinese algae eater grows larger, gets more aggressive, and has a different mouth. A flying fox wears a similar stripe and does not do the same job on black beard. On a true Siamese algae eater the dark stripe continues into the tail, and the mouth is not a big sucker parked under the head. A juvenile in a dealer tank looks smaller than the animal you are buying. At 6 inches it is no longer a nano fish, and 20 gallons is the floor for that length, not a suggestion that it will stay 3 inches.",
    "They are useful against black beard when they are hungry and the algae is the soft new growth, not a foot-long forest that already took over a neglected tank. They also need a normal diet. Algae wafers, blanched greens, and ordinary omnivore foods keep them fed when the beard algae is gone. A keeper who buys one, sees the algae disappear, and then never feeds the fish will have a thin 6-inch cyprinid and a tank that grows algae again the moment the fish slows down. They do not replace water changes.",
    "Beginner care assumes you bought the right species and gave it 20 gallons with a lid. They are active, they rest on leaves and wood at odd angles, and they can be kept singly or with a few of their own kind if the tank is long enough for a group to spread out. Peaceful does not mean they enjoy being trapped with fin-nipping barbs in a short aquarium. It means they are not a territorial cichlid. Fast, busy tankmates of a similar size are a better fit than a slow fancy goldfish or a betta.",
    "The card's money figures are generic and wide: setup about 100 to 500 dollars or more, the fish anywhere from about 5 to 100 dollars or more, annual upkeep about 200 to 500 dollars, and food about 50 to 150 dollars a year. The expensive mistake is not the sticker price. It is paying for a look-alike that will not touch black beard and may outgrow the peaceful label. Ask for Crossocheilus and look at the stripe on the tail before you leave the shop.",
    "A 10-year lifespan in 20 gallons or more only works if the adult body was part of the plan. Upgrade the swimming length as the fish passes 4 inches if the original tank was the bare minimum with other stock already in it. They are good jumpers when the lid is an afterthought. Cover the tank, feed them after the algae is under control, and do not expect a Chinese algae eater or a flying fox to honor this job description.",
  ],

  "fish/silver-dollar": [
    "Silver dollars are schooling herbivores in Metynnis, with Myloplus used for closely related fish that are sold under the same common name. They are relatives of the pacu, scaled down but not small. Adults reach 5 to 8 inches and live 10 to 15 years. The minimum tank is 75 gallons for a school of five or more. They are peaceful, rated easy to moderate, and they will eat the plants. A planted aquascape and a silver dollar school are opposing projects.",
    "Temperature is 75 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness 4 to 15 dGH. Herbivore waste from a school of five should not push nitrate to 30 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are the baseline for a fish that eats greens all day and produces the waste to match. Five adults in 75 gallons is the card's school, not one fish in a 30-gallon tank hoping for company later. A single silver dollar is nervous, pales, and spends its time at the back glass. The group needs open water as well as length.",
    "Feed them as herbivores first. Blanched lettuce, spinach, zucchini, and herbivore pellets should be the normal meal. They will also take flakes and some frozen foods. If the only greens in the tank are the ones you planted, those are the greens that get eaten. Java fern and tough leaves sometimes survive. Soft stems generally do not. Plan a tank of wood, rock, and open swimming space, and put the salad in on purpose.",
    "They are peaceful toward fish that are too big to be snacks and too calm to shred fins. Avoid tiny juveniles and avoid slow fish with elaborate fins if the school is large and rowdy at feeding time. Easy to moderate means the water recipe is ordinary South American freshwater and the difficulty is the adult school: 75 gallons, five or more fish, and a filter that can handle herbivore waste for a decade or more. The 10-to-15-year lifespan is a real housing commitment.",
    "Juveniles are round, silver, and cheap, and they look like a fish that might stay the size of a tetra. Measure the plan against 8 inches and a group of five. If that group will not fit in 75 gallons with room to turn, do not start with three fish in a smaller tank. They are not a substitute for a pacu and they are not a piranha. They are plant-eating characins that happen to share a family resemblance with both, and the plants will always lose.",
  ],

  "fish/swordtail": [
    "Swordtails are Xiphophorus hellerii, livebearers from Mexico to Honduras. Adults are listed at 4 to 5 inches, and the male is the one that grows the sword, a lower tail extension that makes him look longer than a female of the same body. Adults typically live 3 to 5 years while producing brood after brood. The adults need 20 gallons before the fry are even counted. They are easy omnivores, peaceful overall, with the note that males can be competitive. That competition is real in a short tank with two mature males and no break in the line of sight.",
    "Water is hard and alkaline: 72 to 79°F, pH 7.0 to 8.4, hardness 12 to 30 dGH. Nitrate is tolerated under 40 ppm on this card. The nitrogen cycle still has to show ammonia at 0 ppm and nitrite at 0 ppm. Soft rainwater tanks that suit wild tetras are a poor match. If your tap water is hard, you are already closer to this fish than to a blackwater species. If it is not, raise the mineral content and test it rather than pouring salt in at random. The swordtail's preference is hardness and a moderate temperature, not a brine tank.",
    "They are livebearers, and they produce more fry than a small tank can hold. A female drops large broods and can continue after she is separated from males. A 20-gallon aquarium with a pair will not stay a pair. Fry hide in plants for a while, then they grow, and the nitrate number climbs toward that 40 ppm ceiling. Harvest young to another tank if you want them, or stock fewer adults than the gallon rule of thumb suggests and let the plants and the adults account for some of the surplus.",
    "They hybridize with platys. Both are Xiphophorus, and a mixed community produces fry that do not belong cleanly to either species. Keep them apart if you care what the young are. Inside the swordtails themselves, choose males with intact swords and females that are not being run ragged. A 20-gallon tank can hold a small group if it is longer than it is tall. Several adult males in that footprint will tear swords and stress the females even though the species is called peaceful.",
    "Feed flakes, spirulina, and frozen foods. They eat at the surface and in the middle and they are not picky, which helps the easy rating and hurts the water if you feed until the sword is wagging over a pile of leftovers. The 3-to-5-year lifespan is typical, not a failure, for a fish that breeds this hard. Give the adults 20 gallons, hard water inside the ranges above, and a plan for more babies than the tank can raise. A male with no sword yet is often just a young male. Give him the length to grow the extension before you decide the fish is a female.",
  ],

  "fish/tiger-barb": [
    "Tiger barbs, Puntigrus tetrazona, are small, fast cyprinids from Sumatra and Borneo. Adults are about 2.5 to 3 inches and live 5 to 7 years. A nipping school belongs in 30 gallons or more. Care is easy to moderate. The temperament line says semi-aggressive and fin nipper, and that is the fact that should decide the stocking. They are not a playful alternative to a harlequin in a betta tank.",
    "They do better in a group of six or more so the nipping stays inside the group. Five tiger barbs in a tank with an angelfish will use the angelfish as the outlet. Eight tiger barbs in a 30-gallon tank with similarly robust, short-finned neighbors will mostly chase each other. The 30-gallon minimum is for that group, not for a pair. A smaller tank makes the chasing constant because there is no length to break it up.",
    "Temperature is 74 to 82°F, pH 6.0 to 7.5, and hardness 5 to 15 dGH. The nitrate ceiling for this group is under 30 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are required even though the fish looks tough. They are active omnivores and they eat eagerly: flakes, pellets, frozen foods, and greens. Eager eating in a tight school loads the water. A 30-gallon tank with eight growing barbs needs a filter and a water-change habit sized for that, or the moderate half of the care rating shows up as disease after the easy half convinced you they were indestructible.",
    "Color forms, green and albino included, nip the same way. Cherry barbs, Puntius titteya, are the calmer relative people sometimes wanted when they bought tigers for the stripes. If the tank holds long fins and cannot hold a group of six, the cherry is the barb that fits that job. Tiger barbs themselves should not be kept with bettas, fancy guppies, or angelfish. Choose active fish with short fins, or give the barbs a species tank.",
    "They spawn in fine plants if the group is well fed and the water is in range, and the adults will eat the eggs. Raising fry is a choice, not part of keeping the school. Managing the group is not. Buy the six together so you are not adding one bright fish to a school that already has a target. Leave no gap at the rim, because a chased barb will use it. A startled tiger barb will clear a low rim. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is reasonable for a school that was given 30 gallons, six or more of its own, and nitrate held under 30 ppm.",
  ],

  "fish/turbo-snail": [
    "Turbo snails in the aquarium trade are marine turban snails kept for algae, commonly animals in the genus Turbo, at a size of about 2 to 3 inches. Fed, and kept in stable salt, they live 3 to 5 years. One turbo can be housed in 10 gallons of finished saltwater. Care is rated beginner, the temperament peaceful, and the diet herbivore. The water is saltwater: 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm, ammonia 0 ppm, and nitrite 0 ppm. They are not mystery snails and they will not live in a freshwater planted tank.",
    "A beginner rating assumes the saltwater is already mixed and stable. A turbo snail dropped into a tank that is still cycling, with ammonia above 0 ppm, dies and then makes the ammonia worse. They also starve in a spotless aquarium. Film and hair algae are the food. If the glass is wiped every day and the rocks are new, feed algae wafers or dried seaweed on a clip. A 2-inch snail in a 10-gallon tank with no algae and no supplement does not last the 3-to-5-year span.",
    "They are strong and clumsy. A turbo can bulldoze a loose frag plug, knock a small coral off a rock, and plow a sand bed onto a low polyp. Glue down anything fragile, and do not use them as a precision cleanup tool in a carefully set aquascape. One snail in 10 gallons is plenty. Several large turbos in a small tank will strip the film, then starve, and they add more waste than a beginner tank's skimmer may be ready for.",
    "The shell needs calcium from the saltwater itself. Salinity that swings below the 1.020 to 1.025 band, or a pH that sags under 8.1, shows up as pitting and a slow animal. They can fall onto the sand and struggle to right an upside-down shell, especially a large one in a deep bed. Set a new snail on the rockwork and check it the next day. A snail that stays sealed and smells foul is dead and should come out. An empty-looking shell with the operculum pulled in may still be resting.",
    "Copper, and any medication that contains it, kills marine snails. Treat fish in a separate tank if you need those products. Hermit crabs will recycle a turbo shell after the snail dies, which is not a reason to keep the two in a tiny tank and hope. Give the snail algae, stable reef-range salt, and a 10-gallon minimum that is not already full of competing grazers. The peaceful label means it will not hunt fish. It does not mean it will leave every coral plug where you glued it.",
  ],

  "fish/upside-down-catfish": [
    "The upside-down catfish is a mochokid, Synodontis nigriventris, and it is not a pictus cat. Adults reach about 3 to 4 inches and the lifespan on this card is 15 years, which is long for a fish this size and long enough to make the housing decision matter. A group needs 20 gallons or more under the leaves they rest beneath. Temperament is peaceful and nocturnal. Difficulty is beginner. The feature everyone notices is real: they swim inverted, belly toward the ceiling, and they will rest under a leaf or a piece of wood in that posture.",
    "One upside-down catfish is a wasted purchase. It hides until you forget you own it. Several fish in a 20-gallon tank with broad leaves and dark wood will use the underside of the decor in the open, which is the point of keeping them. They are most active when the room is dim. A tank blasted with light and stripped of cover will look empty at noon and busy after dinner. Feed sinking and midwater foods in the evening: small pellets, frozen bloodworms, and similar omnivore fare. Daytime leftovers from the other fish are not a feeding plan for a 15-year nocturnal group.",
    "They are peaceful toward fish that are not small enough to be eaten. A 4-inch catfish can take a tiny fry or a shrimp. Do not house them with a breeding colony of dwarf shrimp and expect both projects to thrive. Ordinary community tetras of a decent size, peaceful schooling fish, and other calm residents are a better match. They do not have the long sharp spines and the 55-gallon requirement of a pictus, and they should not be stocked as if they did. They also will not clean the glass.",
    "The card's costs are the usual wide band: setup about 100 to 500 dollars or more, each fish about 5 to 100 dollars or more, annual maintenance about 200 to 500 dollars, and food about 50 to 150 dollars a year. Spread across a 15-year lifespan, the annual line matters more than the purchase price. A 20-gallon tank is the floor. A group that will live into its second decade is more comfortable in something longer, with a filter you are willing to maintain after the novelty of the inverted swimming has worn off.",
    "Beginner care fits a cycled tank, a group, and evening feedings. It does not fit a brand-new aquarium and one fish bought as a curiosity. They can be startled into the decor and they appreciate a lid. Provide shade. A pale, skittish animal that never inverts is often a fish with nowhere to tuck under, or a fish that is not S. nigriventris at all. Other Synodontis species swim the right way up and get larger. Buy the one that is already demonstrating the belly-up posture in the shop, and give that group the years the card allows.",
    "Because they live under the wood, a dead fish is easy to miss and a 15-year plan is easy to neglect in year six. Look for every member of the group at feeding time. Gravel vacuums should avoid collapsing the caves they use by day. Peaceful and nocturnal is not the same as invisible and maintenance-free. The inverted swim is the display. The 20-gallon minimum and the decade-plus lifespan are the part you have to keep showing up for.",
  ],

  "fish/vampire-crab": [
    "Vampire crabs are Geosesarma from Java and Sulawesi, small land crabs about 1 to 2 inches across, with a lifespan of 2 to 3 years. Moderate care is the paludarium itself, not a difficult diet. The setup on the card is a paludarium of 10 gallons or more, not a filled aquarium. They drown in a full tank. Most of the box should be land: leaf litter, cork, and soil that stays damp, with a shallow pool of freshwater they can walk out of easily. A deep water column with a floating cork is not the same thing.",
    "The water itself is freshwater, pH 7.0 to 8.0, general hardness 4 to 16 dGH, carbonate hardness 3 to 10 dKH, temperature 72 to 82°F. Ammonia and nitrite in that pool should be 0 ppm, which is tricky because the pool is small and these crabs bury leftovers. Change the pool on a short schedule. Air humidity is 75 to 85 percent. A screen top that lets the land dry out will force the crabs to the water and then fail them there too. Cover most of the lid, mist as needed, and measure humidity instead of guessing from a wet corner.",
    "They climb. A crab on the silicone seam is looking for a way out, and a loose lid is a way out. They also molt on land, hiding until the new shell hardens. A crab that vanishes for a week may be under the leaf litter, not dead. Digging through the soil to check is how legs get torn off. Watch the food instead. If a protein food and a vegetable piece keep disappearing, someone is eating.",
    "The diet is easy to get wrong if you only offer fish flakes on the water. They take insect prey, frozen bloodworms placed on land, and some greens. Breeding is listed as possible in captivity, unlike many brackish crabs. A pair or a small group with enough land, hiding spots, and stable humidity sometimes produces young that stay in the setup. Crowding a 10-gallon paludarium with a pile of males is how limbs are lost. Give each crab a burrow and a line of sight that can be broken.",
    "Do not keep them with fish. The pool is too small for a fish, and the crabs are not an aquatic species that shares a community tank. The 2-to-3-year lifespan is the horizon for an animal that was given land, a shallow freshwater pool, and humid air in the 75 to 85 percent band. A vampire crab sold as a fully aquatic pet has been mislabeled. If the enclosure cannot be mostly land, wait and build that enclosure first.",
  ],

  "fish/white-cloud": [
    "White cloud mountain minnows on this card are very small schooling fish, about 1.5 inches, with a lifespan of 5 to 7 years. An unheated school is at home in 10 gallons. They are peaceful omnivores and are rated beginner. The number that decides whether they thrive is the temperature: 60 to 72°F. A tropical community held at 82°F is too warm for the long term. These are cool-water fish, and a heater left over from a betta setup is often the thing that should be unplugged.",
    "pH can sit anywhere from 6.0 to 8.0, so they are not a blackwater specialist. Cool water is not a license for nitrate at or above 20 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm matter just as much in an unheated tank as in a warm one, and a cold tank does not mean a dirty tank is acceptable. Beginner care is a cycled 10 gallons, a school, and a room that actually stays inside 60 to 72°F. A tank in a sunny window that climbs into the high 70s every afternoon is a warm tank with a cool label.",
    "They are active for their size and they belong in a group. A 10-gallon tank with a school of these 1.5-inch fish, fine plants, and an open lane is a complete setup. Adding them as a dither school to a warm angelfish tank fails the temperature line even if the temperament line says peaceful. Choose other cool-water neighbors if you want company, and check that those neighbors share 60 to 72°F. A single white cloud in a bowl is not the beginner project this card describes.",
    "Feed small omnivore foods. Crushed flake, micro pellets, and the occasional frozen baby brine shrimp are enough. They feed in the upper and middle water and they are not shy once the school is large enough. Overfeeding a 10-gallon tank is the usual way to push nitrate over 20 ppm. Small meals they finish are a better match for a fish this size than a pinch aimed at a goldfish.",
    "The 5-to-7-year lifespan shows up in cool, clean water and disappears in a heated community. If your other fish need the upper 70s, keep the white clouds in their own tank. An air-powered sponge filter is often enough at this bioload and does not add heat. Cap the tank. A 1.5-inch fish still fits through a cable notch. They are small, and a gap at the rim is enough for one to land on the stand. Beginner does not mean a fishbowl on a windowsill. It means an easy fish if you respect 60 to 72°F, 10 gallons, and a real school.",
    "Color is stronger when the fish are cool, mature, and not stressed by a tiny group. Males show more red on the fins. You can breed them in a separate cool tank with fine plants or a mop if you want young, and the adults will eat eggs if they share the space. Breeding is optional. Temperature is not. A white cloud that lives for years in an unheated, stable 10-gallon tank is the ordinary success. A white cloud in a tropical 82°F display is a fish being kept above this card.",
  ],

  "fish/white-cloud-mountain-minnow": [
    "Tanichthys albonubes comes from the White Cloud Mountain area of Guangdong in China, and the fish on this card is that species by name. Adults stay about 1.5 inches, or roughly 4 centimeters, and live 5 to 7 years. The tank is 10 gallons or more. Care is easy. The fish are peaceful, they school, and they eat an ordinary omnivore diet. The temperature line is more specific than a simple tropical range: 60 to 72°F, which is 15 to 22°C, with a note that they can tolerate a wider swing from about 40 to 80°F. Tolerance is not a target. The place they do well for years is the 60 to 72°F band, not a thermostat set near 80°F.",
    "A long stay near 80°F, and certainly a community tank held at a tropical 82°F, sits outside both the preferred band and the stated tolerance. Do not park them in a discus or ram tank and point at the word tolerate. pH is 6.0 to 8.0 and hardness is 5 to 19 dGH, a wide mineral window that lets them live in ordinary tap water that would bother a soft-water tetra. Nitrate on this card should stay under 40 ppm. That ceiling is looser than some other small-fish pages. It is still a ceiling. Ammonia and nitrite do not get a pass because the fish is hardy. Both stay at 0 ppm.",
    "They school. A 10-gallon tank with a planted length and a group large enough to occupy it is the easy setup. One or two fish skulk in the moss and never show the white-and-red fin flash that makes the species worth keeping. They spawn in cool water among fine leaves or a yarn mop, and a separate rearing tank is how you see the fry. The adults are not parental. Easy care includes a lid, because a 4-centimeter fish can still jump, and a filter gentle enough that the school is not pinned to one end of a small tank.",
    "The diet is unfussy omnivore food in small pieces. Flake, micro pellets, and frozen cyclops or baby brine all work. They feed in the open once the group feels safe. Hardness between 5 and 19 dGH means you do not need a remineralizing project unless your water is nearly pure rain. You do need a thermometer. An unheated tank in a house that drops into the 50s at night and a heated tank that never falls below 78°F are both misses for different reasons. Aim at 60 to 72°F and let the tolerance band cover a hot week or a cold room, not the design of the aquarium.",
    "The scientific name matters when you are sorting this fish from other tiny cyprinids sold as mountain minnows. Tanichthys albonubes is the White Cloud Mountain fish from Guangdong, at 1.5 inches and 5 to 7 years, in 10 gallons or more. Gold and long-finned forms are the same species and the same temperature story. They are peaceful with other small, cool-water fish. They are the wrong school to add to a warm, fast tank of tiger barbs. Keep the group together, keep nitrate under 40 ppm, and treat 60 to 72°F as the range you actually set, with 40 to 80°F as the outside edge they can endure and not as the thermostat setting.",
    "A 5-to-7-year life at this size is a quiet success and it depends on not cooking the school. If the only aquarium you run is a lidded tropical tank, choose a different 1.5-inch fish. If you have a cool shelf and 10 gallons, this is one of the easier schooling species on the list, provided the school is big enough to behave like one and the water stays inside the hardness and pH window without drifting into a neglected nitrate pile above 40 ppm.",
  ],

  "fish/wrasse": [
    "Wrasses on this card are saltwater fish, not a single species. Adult size is listed as varying from 3 to 12 inches, lifespan from 5 to 10 years, and the minimum tank as 50 gallons or more. Intermediate is the family rating, and the unnamed species is why. The diet is carnivore. Temperament varies. Temperature is 72 to 82°F, pH 8.1 to 8.4, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, nitrate under 10 ppm, with ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm. Any wrasse you bring home still has to fit inside that envelope, and a 12-inch adult does not fit the same 50-gallon tank as a 3-inch fairy wrasse.",
    "The family is the problem with a generic page. Some wrasses are small, reef-safe pickers of pests. Some bury in the sand at night and need a bed deep enough to disappear into. Some jump. Some get large enough to eat shrimp and small fish. A shop label that only says wrasse is not a care guide. Ask for the genus, then stock the 50-gallon minimum as a floor and raise it when the adult size is toward 12 inches or the fish is a known jumper. A lid belongs on the tank either way. Many wrasses leave through small gaps when the lights go out.",
    "Carnivore feeding means mysis, brine shrimp, and marine pellets, not algae sheets and not the hope that the fish will live on pests alone. A wrasse that refuses prepared food in the shop will not invent an appetite in a new tank. Watch it eat before you buy it, especially if the species has a reputation for being picky. Intermediate care is stable saltwater in the ranges above plus the judgment to match the species to the livestock. A fish that eats ornamental shrimp cannot be added to a reef that is built around those shrimp, even if another wrasse would have been fine.",
    "Temperament varies, so do not stock from the family name. A peaceful fairy wrasse and a territorial six-line are both wrasses and they do not behave alike. One wrasse per tank is a safer default until you know the species is gregarious. Sand-diving species need an open bed of fine sand, not a bare-bottom tank or a crushed-coral carpet that they cannot enter. Without that bed they ricochet off the glass at night and damage the mouth.",
    "The 5-to-10-year lifespan assumes the adult size was housed from the start. A 4-inch juvenile of a species that reaches 12 inches is an upcoming tank upgrade, not a permanent nano fish. Keep nitrate under 10 ppm, salinity steady between 1.020 and 1.025, and the temperature inside 72 to 82°F. If the seller cannot name the wrasse, spend the money on one that is labeled and eating. The generic card can only give you the shared saltwater numbers and the warning that 3 inches and 12 inches are not the same commitment.",
  ],

  "fish/zebra-danio": [
    "Zebra danios are Danio rerio, the small striped fish from India, Pakistan, and Bangladesh that also became a laboratory animal. Adults are about 2 inches and live 3 to 5 years. Fast 2-inch fish need 10 gallons of length at the minimum. Care is rated easy, the diet omnivore, and the temperament peaceful and active. That peaceful label does not make them a gentle nano centerpiece. They are fast, they are busy from dawn until the lights go out, and they nip slow fish with long fins.",
    "The temperature range is 64 to 77°F. They are cool-to-tropical fish, not a match for a tank held in the low 80s. A community at a steady 82°F is above this card. pH is 6.5 to 7.5 and hardness is 5 to 19 dGH. This card allows nitrate under 40 ppm, which is still a limit you have to keep. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are still required. Easy care is a school in 10 gallons or more, inside those numbers, with tankmates that can handle the speed. A betta, a fancy guppy, or an angelfish in a small tank will be harassed even though the danio is not a cichlid.",
    "Keep them as a group and give them length. A 10-gallon tank is the minimum for fish that sprint, and a longer tank is kinder. They spawn readily, scattering eggs, and they are used in research because they breed on a schedule and the embryos are easy to see. None of that requires you to breed them at home. It does explain why a few danios become a crowd if you give them a breeding mop and then do nothing. Adults eat the eggs. A separate tank is how fry survive.",
    "Feed ordinary omnivore fare: flake, small pellets, and frozen foods. They take it at the surface in a rush. That rush is why slower fish in the same tank look underfed. If you want a calm planted tank with long fins, choose a different 2-inch fish. If you want an active school that can live unheated in a room that stays inside 64 to 77°F, this is a durable choice. The 3-to-5-year lifespan is the normal horizon, not a short life caused by keeping them cool.",
    "They jump. A lid on a 10-gallon tank is cheap compared with a danio on the floor. They also tolerate a range of conditions inside the card and get kept in worse ones, which is how the easy rating spreads. Tolerance is not a reason to combine them with delicate, slow, long-finned fish or to heat them like rams. Stock the school, respect 64 to 77°F, hold nitrate under 40 ppm, and let them be what they are: a fast research fish that does well in plain water and poorly as a quiet companion for a betta.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/bangai-cardinal": [
    "Banggai cardinalfish, Pterapogon kauderni, are small marine cardinalfish from a tiny home range in the Banggai Islands of Indonesia. Adults reach about 3 inches and live 5 to 7 years. A slow, hovering school can use 20 gallons if nobody bullies the food. They are peaceful, slow-moving, and schooling, rated beginner, and they are reef safe. The honest buying decision sits next to those easy numbers: the wild population is endangered, and the responsible fish is captive-bred. The card prices captive-bred animals around 15 to 30 dollars, against a setup of about 200 to 400 dollars.",
    "They are mouthbrooders. The male holds the eggs and then the young in his mouth, and a brooding male looks as if he has given up on food. He has not become ill by default. He is carrying a clutch, and he should not be netted, mixed into a crowded dealer tank, or starved of cover while he does it. Captive-bred pairs in a quiet 20-gallon tank with rockwork will sometimes raise young. Wild-caught adults from a declining endemic range are the fish this page is asking you not to treat as a disposable school.",
    "Slow eaters get bullied. Faster tankmates, including many damsels and wrasses, take mysis and pellets before a Banggai has left its hovering spot among the rocks. Feed carnivore foods they can actually catch: mysis, brine shrimp, and small pellets, placed near the group after pushier fish have been fed or in a tank where the cardinals are the main event. A school in 20 gallons works better than one shy fish. They are not active swimmers that need a long sprint lane. They need places to hover without being chased off the food.",
    "Saltwater on this card is 72 to 82°F and a specific gravity of 1.020 to 1.025. pH sits at 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm is the reef target, with a looser under-20 ppm figure for a fish-only system. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH, calcium of 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium of 1250 to 1350 ppm are the mineral background if corals share the tank. Ammonia and nitrite have nowhere to hide at anything above 0 ppm. A new 20-gallon aquarium that has not finished cycling will kill a mouthbrooding cardinal faster than it kills a damsel.",
    "Beginner care is fair for a captive-bred group that is already eating and is not housed with bullies. It is a poor description of a wild Banggai added to a brand-new reef to fill a empty corner. Give them 20 gallons, caves that let a male brood without being harassed, and food that reaches a slow fish. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is realistic for that setup. A pale, thin cardinal in a tank of fast eaters is not a fish that needs a new supplement. It needs a quieter feeding plan, or a different tank.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/blue-damsel": [
    "Chrysiptera cyanea, the blue damselfish, is the cheap bright fish that convinces people a marine tank is easy. Adults are about 3 inches and live 5 to 6 years. House a blue damsel in 30 gallons or larger. The printed care level is beginner, which describes hardiness rather than manners. The diet is omnivore, flakes and pellets and frozen foods, and the fish is reef safe. Temperament is where the card stops being cute: semi-aggressive to aggressive. A juvenile that dashes around a dealer tank becomes a bully that owns the rockwork and meets every new fish at the glass.",
    "They are hardy, which is why they are stocked first and why that is often a mistake. A blue damsel in a cycling 30-gallon tank may live through ammonia that would kill a cardinal or a wrasse, and it will then spend years attacking the fish you actually wanted. Add it last if you add it at all, into a tank that already has its quieter residents established, and give it enough rock that it can hold a territory without patrolling the entire footprint. One damsel is plenty. A group of Chrysiptera in 30 gallons is a series of fights.",
    "Temperature on this card is a bit tighter than some marine pages, 72 to 78°F, with salinity at 1.020 to 1.025. Hold pH between 8.1 and 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm suits a reef, and under 20 ppm is the fish-only allowance. The purchase price is about 3 to 8 dollars, with a setup around 200 to 400 dollars. That price is why the fish ends up in tanks that are too small and too new. Hardiness does not shrink the 30-gallon minimum or the aggression.",
    "Feed them like the omnivores they are so they are not hunting out of boredom alone. They will still harass. Flakes, pellets, and frozen foods are all accepted, usually with more enthusiasm than the delicate fish in the same tank. Do not use a blue damsel as a test animal for a new aquarium and then expect to rehome it easily. Adults that have held a territory are hard to place. The beginner label fits a keeper who understands a 3-inch bully, not a keeper who wanted a peaceful blue fish.",
    "Reef safe on the card means they are not coral eaters in the way an angelfish is. It does not mean they are safe for every tankmate. Shrimp, small gobies, and new arrivals get charged. If the display is a calm reef with a firefish and a goby, leave the damsel out. The 5-to-6-year lifespan is long enough for a bad stocking choice to define the tank. Buy the fish for the adult temperament, in 30 gallons or more, at 72 to 78°F, or spend the 3 dollars on something that stays as peaceful as it looked in the cup.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/cleaner-wrasse": [
    "The bluestreak cleaner wrasse, Labroides dimidiatus, is a small Indo-Pacific wrasse of about 3 to 4 inches, with a listed lifespan of 4 years or more. The minimum tank is 55 gallons and the care level is expert. It is peaceful, reef safe, and an obligate cleaner. The diet line says parasites from other fish first, and prepared foods only if the individual is trained. The card also says this fish is not recommended for most aquarists. That warning is the care guide.",
    "In the ocean it runs a station where other fish stop and let it pick at their skin and gills. A home tank does not reproduce a day-long parade of clients. Most cleaner wrasses starve. They ignore mysis and pellets, grow thin along the back, and die while the rest of the tank still looks fed. Watching one eat prepared food several times in the shop is the minimum evidence. A fish that only pecks at tankmates in the dealer display is showing you the problem, not a party trick.",
    "It is not an ich cure. Marine ich is a parasite with a life cycle that a single wrasse cannot break by picking at a few white spots. Buying a cleaner wrasse to treat a sick tank adds a starving fish to a sick tank. Quarantine, the right diagnosis, and a treatment plan that does not depend on another animal are how ich is handled. A utility fish that usually dies is not a tool. Do not recommend one, and do not accept one, as a shortcut around husbandry.",
    "If an experienced system already has a cleaner that is eating frozen foods, the numbers on the card still apply. Temperature is 72 to 78°F. Specific gravity belongs between 1.020 and 1.025. Keep pH in the 8.1 to 8.4 band if you keep one at all. Nitrate should sit under 10 ppm on a reef and under 20 ppm in a fish-only tank. Alkalinity from 8 to 12 dKH, calcium from 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium from 1250 to 1350 ppm belong to the reef around it, not to a special wrasse recipe. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are assumed. The tank is 55 gallons because the fish ranges and advertises, not because it will stay in a nano as a medic.",
    "The listed price, about 15 to 30 dollars, is low enough to look like a bargain remedy. It is a low price for an animal that usually cannot be kept. Expert care here means refusing the purchase unless the fish is already taking prepared food and the tank is a mature 55 gallons with no expectation that it will deworm the display. Captive-bred cleaners are rarely the fish in the cup. A wild obligate cleaner is a wild animal with a job your aquarium does not provide.",
    "Other wrasses clean opportunistically and still eat a normal carnivore diet. They are the ones to consider if the goal is a wrasse. Labroides dimidiatus is the one to leave in the ocean unless its next meal is already frozen food it has proven it will take. A thin cleaner in a home tank is not a training project for a beginner. It is the outcome the card is trying to prevent.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/clownfish": [
    "The clownfish on this card is the ocellaris, a small Indo-Pacific anemonefish about 3 to 4 inches long with a lifespan of 6 to 10 years. A captive-bred pair can live in 20 gallons without an anemone. Beginner is accurate for captive-bred ocellaris in a tank that has already cycled. The diet is omnivore: pellets, frozen mysis, and flakes. Temperament is semi-aggressive and hardy. Reef safe comes with a caution around anemones. Captive-bred fish are the ones to buy, listed around 15 to 40 dollars, with a setup in the 200 to 500 dollar range. A wild clownfish is an unnecessary take for a species the hobby already breeds.",
    "They do not require an anemone. A pair will host a bubble-tip if you insist, and they will just as readily adopt a rock, a corner of a powerhead, or a patch of leather coral. The caution on the card is there because an anemone is a stinging animal with its own needs, not a toy that comes with the fish. Many anemones in new reefs wander, bleach, or eat the clown. Skip the anemone until the 20-gallon tank has been stable for a long time, and skip it entirely if the goal is simply to keep the fish.",
    "Ocellaris form a hierarchy. The largest fish becomes the female, the next becomes the breeding male, and any smaller fish stays suppressed. Two large fish added together in 20 gallons often fight. A better start is a pair that is already together, or a larger fish and a clearly smaller one. They will thump tankmates that swim through the chosen host site. Semi-aggressive is accurate once they have a territory, and it is milder than a maroon clown, which is a different species and not this page.",
    "Temperature can run 72 to 82°F and salinity 1.020 to 1.025. Keep pH at 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm is the reef mark and under 20 ppm is the fish-only mark. They are hardy beginners only after the tank is past the cycle. Ammonia above 0 ppm still burns gills, and nitrite above 0 ppm is the same kind of failure. Feed pellets and mysis on a schedule. They overeat and they beg, and a 4-inch female in a 20-gallon tank produces more waste than the juvenile suggested.",
    "A lid is optional for some marine fish and wiser for a clown that has been known to launch during a water change. Give them a host site that is not the intake of a filter. The 6-to-10-year lifespan is ordinary for captive-bred ocellaris in stable salt. It is not a reason to add a wild anemone, a third adult, or a maroon clown to the same 20 gallons. One established pair, food they already accept, and water inside those ranges is the beginner tank this card is describing.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/copperband-butterfly": [
    "Chelmon rostratus, the copperband butterflyfish, is a specialized Indo-Pacific feeder with a long snout, an adult size around 8 inches, and a lifespan of 5 to 10 years. An 8-inch copperband needs 75 gallons of rock to forage across. Expert is the rating because so many of them never eat. Temperament is peaceful, and the feeding line is the risk: they are finicky. Many never take a normal aquarium diet. The card lists Aiptasia, mysis, frozen foods, and clams, and it notes that the fish requires an established tank with live food sources. A copperband that is not eating is not a fish you train with optimism. It is a fish that starves.",
    "The only honest recommendation is a captive-bred animal or a fish that is already eating in front of you. Wild copperbands often refuse pellets, refuse mysis, and pick at rockwork until the body thins behind the head. If the shop cannot show this individual taking frozen food, walk away. Buying one to clear Aiptasia, then discovering it will not eat at all, solves neither the anemones nor the hunger. Aiptasia on the diet list is a bonus for a fish that is already feeding, not a job description for a new arrival.",
    "They nip coral and anemones. The card's reef-safe line is caution, specifically clam mantles, and the behavior extends to coral tissue and to ornamental anemones. A reef built around expensive LPS and a tridacna clam is the wrong place to experiment. A large fish-only tank with live rock, or a reef you are willing to see sampled, is the honest layout. Peaceful refers to other fish. It does not refer to sessile invertebrates.",
    "Seventy-five gallons is the minimum for an 8-inch fish that forages all day across the rock. For a copperband that is eating, hold 72 to 82°F, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, and pH 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm on a reef and under 20 ppm in a fish-only system, with alkalinity 8 to 12 dKH, calcium 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium 1250 to 1350 ppm when corals are present. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are part of expert care because a stressed, new copperband stops eating at the first swing. The listed cost is about 30 to 60 dollars, which is not enough money to justify a fish that may never feed.",
    "Give a confirmed eater several small offerings of mysis, clam, and other frozen foods through the day. A single sprinkle at lights-on is easy to miss for a slow, snout-feeding animal. House it with calm fish. Damsels and aggressive tangs will keep it off the rocks and the weight will fall off. Expert means the tank was mature before the fish arrived, the fish was already eating, and you are prepared to rehome it or return it at the first week of refusal rather than waiting for a rescue feeding that does not come.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/coral-beauty": [
    "Centropyge bispinosa, the coral beauty, is a dwarf angelfish of about 4 inches with a lifespan of 5 to 7 years. A coral beauty needs 70 gallons of mature rock to graze. Intermediate care is one angel and a reef you can stand to see sampled. It is a hardy dwarf angel by angel standards, semi-aggressive, and an omnivore that should get spirulina, mysis, and foods made for angelfish. The reef-safe line is with caution because it may nip corals. That caution is the correct confidence level. This is not a fish to call reef safe and then be surprised by a pecked LPS.",
    "They graze film on the rocks. A mature 70-gallon reef with algae on the back surfaces suits that habit better than a sterile new tank full of dry rock. Grazing is also how they find coral polyps and clam mantles to sample. Some individuals never touch a coral. Some do it every night. You cannot see which one you bought until it is in your tank, so stock it only where a nipped coral is an acceptable loss. One coral beauty per tank. A second Centropyge in 70 gallons is a territorial fight more often than a pair.",
    "Semi-aggressive shows up toward other angels, toward new fish, and sometimes toward shrimp. Add the angel after quieter fish have settled, or accept that a coral beauty already in residence will meet the next introduction with a charge. They are not maroons and they are not damsels, but the cute juvenile in the shop is still a dwarf angel with a territory. Seventy gallons gives that territory somewhere to be besides the entire aquascape.",
    "Temperature runs 72 to 82°F and salinity 1.020 to 1.025. pH of 8.1 to 8.4, nitrate under 10 ppm in a reef and under 20 ppm if the tank is fish-only, alkalinity held at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm are the water the card expects. Ammonia has to stay at 0 ppm, with nitrite matching it, or the grazing stops. The purchase price is about 30 to 60 dollars and the setup about 300 to 500. None of those figures buy a guarantee that your particular fish will ignore corals.",
    "Feed angelfish preparations and spirulina so the film on the rocks is not the only meal. A hungry dwarf angel nips more. A fed one still might. Intermediate care is a 70-gallon tank, a single specimen, and a reef you are willing to police each evening for fresh white pecks on coral tissue. If the display cannot lose a polyp, choose a fish that does not carry this caution. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is plenty of time for a casual nipper to reshape a garden.",
    "Color varies from deep blue and orange to paler fish, and color is not a behavior test. Watch the mouth, not the poster. A coral beauty that spends the day picking at rock film and prepared food is doing the job you can actually expect. One that settles onto a particular coral every afternoon should be moved to a fish-only system with live rock rather than debated with the reef.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/dottyback": [
    "The dottyback this card is built around is the orchid, Pseudochromis fridmani, a Red Sea endemic of about 3 inches. The lifespan is 5 to 7 years. An orchid dottyback starts at 30 gallons. Care is rated beginner, the fish is reef safe on the card, and the temperament is semi-aggressive and territorial. Captive-bred orchids are the normal fish to buy, around 20 to 40 dollars, with a setup near 200 to 400 dollars. Even this, the usual beginner species in the family, eats small shrimp and small fish.",
    "That diet line is easy to skip because the card also says they hunt bristleworms and take mysis, brine shrimp, and pellets. They do all of that. A hungry or simply opportunistic orchid will also take a sexy shrimp, a small goby, or a juvenile clown that wanders into the cave. Beginner does not mean safe for a nano reef full of ornamental shrimp. It means a hardy, captive-bred fish that eats prepared foods and holds a territory in 30 gallons.",
    "Keep one. Two orchids in a 30-gallon tank will fight until one is ruined, unless they are a proven bonded pair and even then the tank is tight. They need caves. A pile of rock with a real hole, not a flat sculpture, is the furniture. They back into the hole and meet intruders at the door. Keep an orchid at 72 to 82°F, with specific gravity 1.020 to 1.025 and pH between 8.1 and 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm for a reef and under 20 ppm for fish-only water, with ammonia and nitrite held at 0 ppm. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH alongside calcium at 380 to 450 ppm and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm is the reef context, not a special dottyback formula.",
    "Feed them so the bristleworm hunting stays a side effect. Pellets and mysis on a schedule reduce, and do not erase, the chance they eat something you valued. They are poor tankmates for a mandarin that has to hunt copepods in the open, and poor tankmates for a firefish that shares the same cave. Semi-aggressive is all day, not only at feeding time. A lid helps. Dottybacks jump less than wrasses and still find a gap during an argument.",
    "Other Pseudochromis are meaner. If the fish was not sold as an orchid or as P. fridmani, do not apply the beginner label from this card to a sunrise or a magnificent dottyback. Those are different animals. For the orchid itself, 30 gallons, one fish, captive-bred, no treasured shrimp, and a cave it can own is the setup that matches the card. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is likely. The shrimp's lifespan in the same tank is the open question.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/emperor-angelfish": [
    "Pomacanthus imperator is a large Indo-Pacific angelfish, about 15 inches as an adult, with a lifespan of 15 to 20 years. The floor for one emperor is 220 gallons. Expert care is the size, the sponge foods, and the reef you are willing to lose. Semi-aggressive, at this size, means one emperor owns the tank. Reef safe is listed as no, because the fish eats corals and sponges. The juvenile pattern is a series of blue and white circles that disappear as the fish becomes the barred yellow-and-blue adult. Buying the juvenile for the pattern and housing it as if it will stay a small reef fish is the central mistake.",
    "Use the page's gallon number. Two hundred and twenty gallons is the floor for one emperor, not a stretch goal after it outgrows a 75. A 15-inch angelfish needs swimming length and rockwork it cannot demolish. Filtration and water changes have to match a heavy, constantly grazing body for up to two decades. The listed cost is about 100 to 300 dollars for the fish and 600 dollars or more for a large setup. The setup figure is the one that matters. A bargain juvenile in a tank under 220 gallons is an animal already scheduled for a move.",
    "Adults are not reef safe. They eat coral and sponges, and a fish that ignored LPS as a juvenile often changes its mind as the pattern changes. Plan a fish-only system with live rock, or a tank where the corals are not the point. Diet on the card is omnivore with sponge-based angelfish food, mysis, spirulina, and nori. Offer those foods early and often so grazing the aquascape is not the only way it gets a meal. Sponge in the prepared food matters for this genus. A flake-only diet thins a large angel even when it looks busy.",
    "Run that large system at 72 to 82°F and a specific gravity of 1.020 to 1.025. Keep pH at 8.1 to 8.4, ammonia at 0 ppm, and nitrite at 0 ppm. Nitrate under 20 ppm is the fish-only allowance. The under-10 ppm reef figure is academic if the fish is eating the reef. Alkalinity, calcium, and magnesium targets of 8 to 12 dKH, 380 to 450 ppm, and 1250 to 1350 ppm matter when the tank still has invertebrates you intend to keep. Semi-aggressive means one emperor, not a pair, and not a mix with other large Pomacanthus unless the system is far beyond 220 gallons.",
    "The pattern change is gradual. A fish that is half juvenile and half adult is not sick. It is growing into the 15-inch animal the tank had to be built for. Expert care is the size, the diet with sponge foods, and the refusal to put it on a reef you care about. The 15-to-20-year lifespan makes a wrong tank a long problem. If 220 gallons is not available now, it is not available for this species.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/fairy-wrasse": [
    "Fairy wrasses are the small, colorful Cirrhilabrus and their close allies, listed here as a group rather than one species. Adults run about 3 to 5 inches and live 5 to 8 years. Fairy wrasses need 55 gallons of open water above the rock. Intermediate care comes from the lid and the repeated small feedings. They are peaceful, active swimmers, and reef safe. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, copepods, and pellets. Cost depends on the species, roughly 30 to 100 dollars, with a setup around 300 to 500. A rare male is not a different care category. He is a more expensive fish with the same need to eat and the same need for a lid.",
    "Males flash at each other and at females, shooting color across the body when they display. That display needs open water above the rock, which is why 55 gallons of length matters more than a tall cube of the same volume. One male with females, or a single fish, is the peaceful version. Two adult males of the same species in 55 gallons will display until it becomes harassment. Know the sex and the species before you add a second.",
    "They jump. A fairy wrasse can leave through a small feeding hole when the lights switch or when a male is chased. Cover the tank completely, including gaps around plumbing. They also need to eat small foods several times a day in a busy reef, because they are not the fish that wins a single feeding against a tang. Mysis, enriched brine, and pellets they recognize are the staple. Copepods on the rock are a supplement, not a plan, unless the tank is mature and the wrasse is one of the smaller species.",
    "Temperature on this card is 72 to 78°F, slightly cooler at the top end than a general 82°F reef recipe. Hold the salt between 1.020 and 1.025 for these swimmers. The pH window for a fairy wrasse tank is 8.1 to 8.4. A reef should keep nitrate under 10 ppm, while a fish-only system can allow under 20 ppm. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm, alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm round out a tank that may also hold corals. Intermediate care is the lid, the feeding frequency, and the choice of one male.",
    "Reef safe here means they do not graze coral the way an angel does. They may still eat tiny mobile invertebrates. A reef of SPS and LPS with a fairy wrasse is a standard combination. A tank built around a rare copepod-dependent fish may not want the extra hunter. The 5-to-8-year lifespan fits a covered 55-gallon tank and a fish that was eating before you bought it. A male that fades and hides is usually a second male, a missing lid, or a feeding plan that never reaches him.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/firefish": [
    "Nemateleotris magnifica is a dartfish, sold as a firefish or a firefish goby, and it does not behave like a goby that sits in the open on the sand. Adults are about 3 inches and live 3 to 5 years. A lidded 20-gallon tank is the smallest honest home. The care rating is beginner only after the cover and the cave exist. They are peaceful, timid, and they hover. This dartfish leaves coral polyps alone. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, and finely chopped seafood. The price is about 15 to 30 dollars, with a setup around 200 to 400. The behavior that empties the tank is jumping.",
    "They need a lid and a cave. A firefish hovers a short distance from a burrow and dives backward into it when anything passes. Without the burrow it hangs at the surface and looks for a way out. Without the lid it finds one. A rimless 20-gallon tank with a gap for a heater cable is enough of an exit. Cover every opening. The cave can be a rock pile or a short length of pipe aimed so the fish can face outward. A firefish in the middle of a bare tank is a stressed animal, not a bold one.",
    "Do not confuse it with a goby that lives on the sand in full view. The shop name firefish goby is a label, not a description of the family. This fish is a dartfish. It will not pair with a pistol shrimp, it will not sift sand, and it will not become confident because you added more light. It becomes confident when the cave is trustworthy and the tankmates are calm. Damsels, aggressive wrasses, and large hawkfish are how a timid hoverer stops eating.",
    "Temperature on this card tops out at 80°F, listed as 72 to 80°F, with salinity 1.020 to 1.025. Hold the pH at 8.1 to 8.4 beside that burrow. Nitrate under 10 ppm matches a reef. Under 20 ppm is the looser fish-only mark. Alkalinity 8 to 12 dKH, calcium 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium 1250 to 1350 ppm apply if the 20-gallon tank is a reef. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are required. Twenty gallons is a minimum for one fish or a proven pair. Two firefish that are not a pair will fight over the one good cave.",
    "Feed small carnivore portions near the burrow. They miss food that is dumped in a strong current at the far end of the tank. Beginner care is a covered, mature 20-gallon aquarium, a cave, and peaceful neighbors. The 3-to-5-year lifespan is short if the fish spends it jumping or hiding from a bully, and ordinary if the lid and the burrow were there on the first day. A firefish on the carpet is not bad luck. It is an open hole in the cover.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/flame-angelfish": [
    "Centropyge loricula, the flame angelfish, is a dwarf angel from the Pacific, including Hawaii and the Marshall Islands. Adults are about 4 inches and live 5 to 7 years. A flame angel needs 70 gallons before you trust it near corals. The care level is intermediate because the nipping is unpredictable. Semi-aggressive shows up toward other dwarf angels first. The reef-safe line is caution, and the specific problem is nipping at LPS and SPS corals. They also nip clam mantles. One per tank. A second flame angel in 70 gallons is a territorial dispute you will not win with more rock.",
    "They are beautiful enough that the caution gets ignored. A juvenile in a dealer tank is busy, red, and small. The adult holds a patch of reef and tests corals with its mouth, especially large-polyp stones and the edge of a clam. Some individuals are mild. The card does not let you assume you bought one of those. Stock a flame angel where coral damage is survivable, or keep it in a fish-only tank with live rock and heavy feeding. Calling it reef safe with confidence is how a clam mantle disappears.",
    "The diet is omnivore: spirulina, mysis, angelfish formula, and algae. Feed those on purpose so grazing is not the whole intake. A fed angel still nips. An underfed one nips more and loses color. Semi-aggressive also applies to other dwarf angels and to new fish that cross the territory. Add the flame angel after the shy fish, not before, and do not add it to a tank that already has a coral beauty unless the system is far larger than 70 gallons.",
    "Keep a flame angel at 72 to 82°F and salinity from 1.020 to 1.025. pH should read 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm is the reef number and under 20 ppm is the fish-only number. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH, calcium of 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium of 1250 to 1350 ppm are the coral-side targets you are protecting if you insist on keeping this fish among LPS. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are the fish-side targets. The purchase price runs about 60 to 120 dollars, with a setup around 300 to 500. The fish costs more than a coral beauty and nips for the same family reasons.",
    "Seventy gallons is the minimum, not a suggestion to try a 40 and upgrade if the corals suffer. One fish, a lid, and a feeding habit that includes spirulina and mysis are the intermediate work. The 5-to-7-year lifespan will outlast a reef that was not supposed to be on the menu. If the display is a clam and a garden of LPS, pick a different fish. The flame angel is a dwarf angel first and an ornament second.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/flame-hawkfish": [
    "The flame hawkfish is a small perching predator from the western Pacific, about 3 to 4 inches, with a lifespan of 5 to 7 years. One perching flame hawkfish needs 30 gallons as a floor. Beginner on the card means ordinary marine care, not a shrimp-safe fish. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, small pellets, and small crustaceans. The temperament line for this perch hunter is semi-aggressive. Reef safe comes with a caution because it eats small shrimp and crabs. The price is about 30 to 60 dollars and the setup about 200 to 400. The fish sits on a rock and watches, which looks calm until something small enough to swallow walks underneath.",
    "Hawkfish do not have a swim bladder that lets them hover the way a wrasse does. They perch, then launch. Give this one a stable rock pile with ledges at several heights, not a bare sand field. It will choose a favorite spot and defend the view. A second hawkfish in 30 gallons, flame or otherwise, usually means one fish gets the perch and the other gets harassed. Beginner care is one fish, meaty foods, and no ornamental shrimp you intend to keep.",
    "Small fish are also food if they fit in the mouth. A tiny goby, a new firefish, or a juvenile clown can be taken. Stock it with fish that are too big to be a meal and bold enough not to be pinned in a corner. It will leave most corals alone and still wreck a cleanup crew of sexy shrimp or emerald crabs. The caution on the card is the stocking rule. Do not add it to solve a pest crab and then be surprised when the peppermint shrimp is gone too.",
    "The water around that perch should be 72 to 82°F at a salinity of 1.020 to 1.025. pH belongs at 8.1 to 8.4. On a reef, hold nitrate under 10 ppm. A fish-only tank can run under 20 ppm. If the tank still runs calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm, that is for the corals, not because the hawkfish grazes film. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm keep a rugged fish from being rugged in name only. Feed mysis and pellets to the perch. A hawkfish will learn the feeding spot and still hunt between meals.",
    "Thirty gallons is enough for one 4-inch flame hawkfish and tight for that fish plus a community of small, shy species. A lid is wise when the fish launches at a reflection. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is likely if you fed it and did not ask it to live on algae. Beginner means the water and the food are ordinary marine carnivore care. It does not mean the fish is safe around the shrimp that make a reef look finished.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/foxface-rabbitfish": [
    "Siganus vulpinus, the foxface rabbitfish, is a western Pacific herbivore that reaches about 8 to 10 inches and lives 5 to 7 years. An 8-to-10-inch foxface needs 75 gallons of length to cruise. Intermediate care includes the venom as much as the algae. Temperament is peaceful, with the job description of an algae grazer, and reef safe is listed with caution because it may nip soft corals. The price is about 30 to 60 dollars and the setup about 300 to 500. The fact that should come before the algae praise is the venom. The dorsal, anal, and pelvic spines can puncture a hand. A sting is painful and it happens when a fish is netted, bagged, or trapped against the glass.",
    "Handle a foxface with a container, not a loose net and a squeeze. Do not put your hand into a cave it has entered. The venom is a defense, and a calm fish in the open is not a tame fish when it is cornered. Tell anyone who maintains the tank. Intermediate care includes that respect, plus a 75-gallon swimming tank. They are active grazers and they need length. A tall tank that is short from front to back turns an 8-inch rabbitfish into a pacing animal.",
    "They eat hair algae, and they also eat some coral. Nori on a clip, spirulina, algae pellets, and blanched vegetables should be in the tank every day so the soft corals are not the salad. The caution is real. A foxface that ignores LPS for months can still strip a leather or a zoanthid patch when it is hungry or when the nori runs out. Peaceful applies to fish. It does not apply to every sessile invertebrate, and it does not apply to your hand.",
    "Keep the swimming tank at 72 to 82°F, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, and pH 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm on a reef and under 20 ppm in a fish-only system. Alkalinity 8 to 12 dKH, calcium 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium 1250 to 1350 ppm are the reef numbers you are spending effort on if corals are present to be nipped. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm matter because a rabbitfish eats a large volume of greens and produces the waste of a herbivore that size. One fish per tank is the usual limit unless the system is huge. They can squabble with their own kind in 75 gallons.",
    "Feed the vegetables even after the hair algae is gone. A foxface that cleaned the rocks and then wasted away was never given a replacement diet. The 5-to-7-year lifespan fits a 75-gallon tank, daily herbivore food, and a keeper who does not learn about the spines during a panicked netting. If the reef cannot lose a soft coral, this is a grazer for a different system. The venom does not fade because the fish is useful.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/green-chromis": [
    "Chromis viridis is a small Indo-Pacific schooling damsel, about 3 inches, and the lifespan on this card is a long 8 to 15 years. The school needs 30 gallons before you add anyone else. Beginner care here means a cycled tank and a full group. They are peaceful, reef safe, and omnivores that take flakes, pellets, mysis, and brine shrimp. The price is low, about 5 to 15 dollars each, with a setup around 200 to 400. They are a school. A pair of green chromis in a new 30-gallon tank is the stocking that fades, gets marine ich, and makes people decide the species is fragile.",
    "Buy a real group, and add them together to a tank that has already finished cycling. A school that is too small spends its time hiding and fighting instead of hovering in the open the way the fish does on a reef. A brand-new aquarium does the same damage from the other direction. Chromis show stress as washed-out color and as ich when the group is undersized or the tank is new. That is a husbandry result, not a reason to treat the species as disposable because each fish was cheap.",
    "Thirty gallons can hold a modest school of 3-inch fish if the tank is long and the filtration is ahead of the bioload. It cannot hold a school plus a pile of other fish and still look like beginner care. They are peaceful toward other species and less peaceful toward a stray chromis added months later. Buy the group once. Replacing them one at a time is how you get a veteran that harasses every newcomer until the school never reforms.",
    "Chromis viridis is kept at 72 to 82°F and a specific gravity of 1.020 to 1.025. pH should sit between 8.1 and 8.4 above the rock. Keep nitrate under 10 ppm if corals share the water and under 20 ppm if the tank is fish-only. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are the cycling lesson this fish is often used to skip and should not be. Alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm matter when the school is in a reef, which is a fair place for them because they are reef safe and they swim above the corals rather than eating them.",
    "The 8-to-15-year lifespan is the surprise in a fish that costs less than a meal. It only shows up if the school stays intact and the tank stays mature. Feed them in the open water so every fish eats, not only the bold one at the front. A green chromis that is dark, thin, and scratching on the sand is a prompt to look at group size and at how new the aquarium is. This page is the marine Chromis viridis school, not a freshwater blue-green chromis with a similar common name and a different salt requirement.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/hawkfish": [
    "The hawkfish on this page is the longnose, Oxycirrhites typus. Adults reach about 5 inches and live 5 to 7 years. A single longnose needs 30 gallons at minimum. The care line says beginner because the saltwater recipe is ordinary. The diet is carnivore, built from mysis, brine shrimp, and small pellets. Temperament is semi-aggressive and perching. Reef safe is caution because the fish eats ornamental shrimp. It comes from the Indo-Pacific, and the shop price is about 30 to 60 dollars against a setup of roughly 200 to 400. The long snout is for pulling small animals out of crevices, not for show.",
    "They perch on gorgonians and on any thin projection that looks like one. A tank with a sea fan, a tall rock spine, or a piece of branching structure gives them the seat they use in the ocean. From that seat they watch shrimp. A cleaner shrimp, a sexy shrimp, or a small crab is a meal, not a coworker. The caution is the whole invertebrate stocking plan. Corals are usually ignored. The cleanup crew is not.",
    "Small fish can disappear for the same reason. A 5-inch longnose has a mouth that matches the snout, and a tiny goby is not safe because the hawkfish looks slender. Choose tankmates with enough body that they are not a lunge away from being eaten. One longnose per 30 gallons. A second hawkfish, even a different species, competes for the perch and the argument is one-sided. Beginner water care does not cancel the predatory part.",
    "Hold the tank at 72 to 82°F and a salinity of 1.020 to 1.025. pH should stay at 8.1 to 8.4, ammonia at 0 ppm, and nitrite at 0 ppm. Nitrate under 10 ppm is the reef line and under 20 ppm is the fish-only line. Calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm are there for the corals and the gorgonian you may have added as a perch. The fish itself needs the meaty foods. It will not graze film the way a tang does, and a pellet-only diet that it refuses should be swapped for mysis it will actually take.",
    "Thirty gallons is the minimum for a single 5-inch fish that spends its life on a stalk of coral or rock. Give it that perch on day one so it is not pacing the glass. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is a reasonable expectation for a longnose that is fed and kept away from a second hawk. It is a poor expectation for the ornamental shrimp in the same aquarium. If the reef's point is the shrimp, this is the wrong perch predator. If the reef's point is corals and larger fish, the longnose is a beginner fish with a clear menu.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/kole-tang": [
    "The kole tang, Ctenochaetus strigosus, is a Hawaiian bristletooth tang of about 7 inches with a lifespan of 5 to 7 years. A single kole needs 75 gallons of swimming length. Intermediate is a fair rating for one bristletooth with a daily nori clip. It is a peaceful, active algae grazer, reef safe on this card, and a herbivore that should get nori, spirulina, algae, and the film it is built to comb. The price runs about 50 to 120 dollars, with a setup around 300 to 500. The mouth is the detail that separates it from a yellow tang. A kole has bristle-like teeth for brushing film off rock. It does not carry a scalpel at the base of the tail in the same way a surgeonfish such as a yellow tang does. It still has a caudal spine. Treat the tail with the same caution you would give any tang when you net it.",
    "One tang per tank unless the tank is huge. A kole in 75 gallons owns the grazing circuit. A second tang, kole or otherwise, in that footprint is a chase that ends with torn fins and a fish that will not come out to eat. Seventy-five gallons is the minimum for a single 7-inch grazer, not a starter size for a tang collection. They swim more than they perch. Length matters. A tall tank without a lap to swim will show as a fish that only circles the heater.",
    "Temperature on this card is 72 to 78°F, cooler at the ceiling than a general reef that is allowed to reach 82. Salinity for this Hawaiian tang is 1.020 to 1.025. Keep the kole inside that cooler band rather than letting a summer tank sit at the top of some other species' range. Keep the kole's pH inside 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm is appropriate because this fish belongs on a reef with film to harvest, and under 20 ppm is the fish-only fallback. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH, calcium of 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium of 1250 to 1350 ppm support the corals it is rated to live with. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are non-negotiable for a tang, which will show stress on the skin before a tougher fish does.",
    "Feed the film and then feed as if the film is not enough. Nori on a clip, spirulina, and algae-based foods every day keep a kole from starving in a tank you have scraped clean because you wanted the rocks purple. They graze constantly. A single daily pinch of flake is a yellow-tang myth applied to the wrong mouth. Reef safe here means the kole is not a coral predator. It can still knock loose frags while it combs, and the caudal spine can catch your hand, a net, or another fish during a scare.",
    "Intermediate care is the 75-gallon minimum, a single tang, constant herbivore food, and stable water in the 72 to 78°F band. The 5-to-7-year lifespan is typical for a kole that was given that grazing life rather than a bare quarantine tank extended into a permanent home. Do not buy it as a yellow tang that stays smaller. The bristle mouth, the Hawaiian distribution, and the spine at the tail are a different animal with a different tool kit and the same need for a large, quiet territory.",
    "A new kole often arrives thin and pale. Give it live rock with real film, a nori clip on the first day, and no second tang to fight. Quarantine is wise for any tang because ich finds them easily, and a 75-gallon display is a bad place to discover spots. The card's peaceful label assumes the fish is the only surgeonfish in the system. It is peaceful toward dissimilar fish. It is not peaceful toward a rival with the same job.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/mandarin-goby": [
    "Synchiropus splendidus, the mandarin dragonet, is a small western Pacific fish of about 3 inches. The lifespan is 2 to 5 years. The minimum tank is 30 gallons of mature reef. Expert is the rating, and it is almost entirely about whether the fish will eat. Temperament is peaceful and slow-moving, and the fish is reef safe. The diet line is the whole difficulty: copepods first, and prepared foods only if the fish has been trained. The price looks mild, about 20 to 40 dollars, and the card's setup note is not a dollar figure. It is a mature tank with a copepod population. Most wild mandarins starve.",
    "A wild dragonet hunts tiny crustaceans all day on the rock and the sand. A new aquarium, a bare dealer tank, or a reef that was scrubbed and stocked the same month does not have those animals in any useful number. The fish picks at the rocks, gets thin behind the head, and dies while a prepared-food feeding happens at the other end of the tank. Expert care starts by not buying that fish. The mandarins that live are captive-bred animals that already eat frozen food, or the rare wild individual you have personally watched take mysis or enriched brine over and over.",
    "Thirty gallons of mature reef is the floor, not a small display you dose with a bottle of copepods once and call finished. Pods have to reproduce faster than the dragonet eats them, which means live rock, a refugium or a quiet sump, and no hungry wrasse competing for the same prey. A six-line or a melanurus in the same 30 gallons can empty the pantry. Peaceful describes the mandarin. It does not describe every tankmate you might add beside it.",
    "A mature mandarin reef should run 72 to 82°F and salinity of 1.020 to 1.025. That same reef should read 8.1 to 8.4 for pH. Nitrate under 10 ppm fits the reef this fish requires. The under-20 ppm fish-only number is a poor target, because a fish-only tank without a pod population is how wild mandarins starve. Alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm are the mature-reef background. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm should have been true for months before this fish arrives, not for a weekend.",
    "Feed a captive-bred, frozen-food eater the same way you proved it in the shop: small mysis or enriched brine several times a day, placed where a slow fish can pick them off the rock. One feeding a day is not the rhythm of a dragonet. The 2-to-5-year lifespan is the upper end for a fish that is eating and the lower end, or worse, for a wild fish living on hope. Reef safe means it will not eat corals. It does not mean any reef is ready for it. If the tank is under a year old and the fish is wild, the expert decision is to leave it.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/maroon-clownfish": [
    "The maroon clownfish is Amphiprion biaculeatus, still widely called Premnas, and it is the largest of the common clownfishes. Adults reach about 6 inches and live 7 years or more. A 6-inch maroon needs 30 gallons at the very least. Intermediate is the rating, and the bite is why. The diet is omnivore: pellets, frozen mysis, brine shrimp, and flakes. Temperament is aggressive and territorial. The card does list them as reef safe. They are not ocellaris. A maroon will bite, and the bite is a real injury to a hand in the tank, not a comic nip from a small orange fish.",
    "They can host in an anemone and they do not require one. A bubble-tip is a project of its own, with light, flow, and a habit of wandering. A maroon will claim a rock or a corner just as readily and will defend that spot against fish that would have been acceptable tankmates for an ocellaris. Thirty gallons is the minimum for one fish or a known pair. It is small once the female is 6 inches and owns the aquascape. Gold-stripe maroons cost more inside the listed 20 to 80 dollar range. The stripe does not change the aggression or the adult size. Setup costs on the card run about 200 to 500 dollars.",
    "Add them with a plan for the hierarchy. A large female and a smaller male can form a pair. Two similar adults often fight until one is dead. They will also charge new fish, shrimp, and the person who cleans the glass. Intermediate care means you accept a territorial 6-inch clown and you do not put your fingers in its cave. Feed pellets and mysis on a schedule so the aggression is not fueled only by hunger, and do not expect feeding to cancel it.",
    "Temperature is 72 to 82°F, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, and pH 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm in a reef and under 20 ppm in a fish-only tank. Alkalinity 8 to 12 dKH, calcium 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium 1250 to 1350 ppm are the reef targets beside a fish the card calls reef safe. Even a hardy maroon needs ammonia at 0 ppm and nitrite at 0 ppm. Hardiness is not the same trait as a mild temper. Maroons survive conditions that kill delicate fish, and they use that stamina to hold a territory you cannot argue with.",
    "A lid and a cave they have chosen are the furniture. Do not keep them with an ocellaris and call it a clownfish pair. Different species under one host is a fight. The 7-year-or-more lifespan is a long time to keep a fish that bites, so choose it on purpose, in 30 gallons or more, with tankmates that can stay out of its hole. If you wanted a peaceful beginner clown, that is the ocellaris page and a different adult size.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/melanurus-wrasse": [
    "Halichoeres melanurus is a western Pacific wrasse of about 5 inches, with a lifespan of 5 to 8 years. Plan on 50 gallons so the sand bed and the swimming room both exist. Beginner is printed on the card for a fish that still needs a lid and sand. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, pellets, and a habit of hunting pests. It stays on the move, and the temper is semi-aggressive toward smaller wrasses. The card marks it reef safe and calls it an excellent pest controller. That is the optimistic half. It is reef-safe-ish in practice, and it may eat small shrimp. A mandarin, a sexy shrimp, or a tiny ornamental crab is not a pest just because the wrasse is useful against flatworms.",
    "They jump, and they bury in the sand to sleep. A lid is required, including the gaps around pipes, because a melanurus will leave through a hole that looks too small when the lights go off. A sand bed is required too. Bare-bottom tanks and crushed coral give them nowhere to dive, and they ricochet off the glass at night and damage the mouth. Fine sand, open enough to enter, is part of the 50-gallon layout. A bed that is only a decorative dusting in one corner is not a bed.",
    "This wrasse is listed at 72 to 78°F, cooler at the top than a general 82°F reef. Keep its salinity between 1.020 and 1.025. pH should remain 8.1 to 8.4 while it hunts the rocks. Nitrate under 10 ppm suits the reef they are usually bought for, and under 20 ppm is the fish-only allowance. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH, calcium of 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium of 1250 to 1350 ppm are the reef side of a tank you hope will keep its corals while this fish works the rocks for pests. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm should already be stable. Beginner refers to a fish that eats and lives in ordinary marine water. It does not refer to a fish you can keep without a cover or a sand bed.",
    "Feed mysis and pellets so pest hunting is extra, not the only calories. A melanurus that is expected to live on pyramidellid snails and flatworms in a sparse tank will get thin and then start sampling whatever small invertebrate is left. Semi-aggressive shows up toward smaller wrasses and toward fish that hover in its path. One per tank in 50 gallons is simpler than a pair. Males and females differ in color, and a male will boss a tank that seemed big enough at the dealer.",
    "The price is about 20 to 40 dollars and the setup about 300 to 500. Spend the setup on the lid and the sand, not only on the fish. The 5-to-8-year lifespan fits a covered 50-gallon tank with a real bed and a carnivore diet. A melanurus on the floor, or a melanurus with a shredded mouth from a bare bottom, is a missing piece of furniture. Reef safe on the label still means you chose it knowing the ornamental shrimp might be part of the pest control.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/powder-blue-tang": [
    "Acanthurus leucosternon, the powder blue tang, is an Indian Ocean surgeonfish of about 9 inches with a lifespan of 10 years or more. The number to build around is 125 gallons for a single fish. Expert care starts in quarantine, not in the display. They swim constantly, and the temper toward other tangs is semi-aggressive. Reef safe here does not make them safe around other surgeonfish. The diet is herbivore: nori, spirulina, algae sheets, and mysis shrimp. The price is about 50 to 100 dollars, and the setup note is 500 dollars or more for a large system. This is not a blue tang. Paracanthurus hepatus is a different fish with a different body and a different reputation. A powder blue is ich-prone and aggressive, and the 125-gallon minimum is the number that belongs on the plan.",
    "Ich finds this species easily. A powder blue that skipped quarantine, came from a crowded system, or went into a tank that was still maturing is a common way to watch white spots spread across a dark blue body. Expert care starts before the fish is in the display: a quarantine period, stable salinity, and no shortcuts because the fish was expensive and looked fine in the bag. This tang is listed cooler than many reef fish, at 72 to 78°F. A hot, swinging reef at the edge of that band adds stress to a fish that already carries this risk. Salinity should stay inside 1.020 to 1.025 without the large fresh-water top-off mistakes that drop it overnight.",
    "Aggression is the other half. One powder blue in 125 gallons can work with dissimilar fish. A second tang in that tank usually cannot. They are semi-aggressive toward other surgeons and they will also harass fish that graze the same rocks. Add the tang last only if the other fish are robust, or give it the tank and choose tankmates that can take a chase. The peaceful yellow tang you might be picturing is not this species. Use the 125-gallon floor and assume the temper is part of the purchase.",
    "Feed herbivore foods all day. Nori on a clip, spirulina, and algae sheets, with mysis as the meaty extra the card includes, keep a 9-inch tang from living only on the film it can strip off the glass. They are active. A 125-gallon tank that is mostly a tall column without a swimming lap will show as constant pacing. pH at 8.1 to 8.4, ammonia at 0 ppm, nitrite at 0 ppm, and nitrate under 10 ppm on a reef or under 20 ppm if the system is fish-only are the water targets. Alkalinity 8 to 12 dKH, calcium 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium 1250 to 1350 ppm support corals beside a fish the card rates reef safe. Reef safe does not mean tang-safe. The caudal spine is a weapon and a netting hazard.",
    "The 10-year-or-more lifespan is why the tank has to be right at the start. A powder blue does not stay a 4-inch juvenile, and it does not become a hepatus if you wished for the other blue fish. Expert means quarantine, 125 gallons, one surgeonfish, constant algae-based food, and salinity that does not swing. Anything less is how a striking Indian Ocean tang becomes an ich outbreak with a short life.",
    "Buy the fish for the adult. A thin powder blue in a shop with clamped fins is already showing you the sensitivity. A fat one in a clean system, eating nori, is the individual worth a 125-gallon commitment. Do not add it to prove a new tank is ready. This is one of the last fish a large reef receives, not the first.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/royal-gramma": [
    "Gramma loreto, the royal gramma, is a small western Atlantic and Caribbean fish of about 3 inches, with a lifespan of 5 to 8 years. One cave-dwelling gramma starts in 30 gallons. Beginner fits a single fish that already has a hole to guard. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, pellets, and copepods. Temperament runs from peaceful to semi-aggressive, and the habit is a cave dweller. They do not pick at coral tissue. The price is about 20 to 40 dollars and the setup about 200 to 400. It is not a royal dottyback. A dottyback is a Pseudochromis with a different shape, a different attitude, and a different family. The gramma is the purple-and-yellow fish that backs into a hole and watches the room.",
    "Caves are the requirement, not a decoration. A royal gramma that has a tight recess in the rock will hover at the entrance and come out to feed. A royal gramma in a tank of smooth, hole-free rock will hide behind equipment and fade. Build at least one cave it can defend before you buy the fish. Two grammas in 30 gallons will fight. They can be kept as a single fish, or as a known pair in a larger system, and two strangers will damage each other even though the species looks mild in a shop photo. Peaceful to semi-aggressive is that split: mild toward other kinds of fish, intolerant of a second gramma in the same small territory.",
    "They are reef safe and they are a poor match for a tank that depends on a dense copepod population, because copepods are on the menu. In an ordinary reef they take mysis and pellets and the pods are extra. Feed those prepared foods to the cave mouth. A gramma will not cross the tank to compete with a tang, and it will slowly thin if every meal is a scramble in the open water. Beginner care is a 30-gallon tank that already has the cave, stable salt, and one fish.",
    "Gramma loreto does well at 72 to 82°F with salinity held at 1.020 to 1.025. Test pH and keep the cave's water between 8.1 and 8.4. Reef nitrate should stay under 10 ppm. Fish-only water can sit under 20 ppm. Alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm are the usual reef minerals beside a fish that leaves corals alone. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm should be old news before a cave dweller is asked to settle. They can jump during an argument or a sloppy water change, so a lid is cheap insurance.",
    "The 5-to-8-year lifespan fits one gramma in a cave it trusts. It does not fit two fish forced to share a 30-gallon hole. If the seller calls the fish a royal dottyback, stop and look at the body. A gramma is purple in front and yellow behind, and it is the fish this card describes. Stock it once, feed the cave, and do not add a second to make the color symmetrical.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/six-line-wrasse": [
    "Pseudocheilinus hexataenia is a small Indo-Pacific wrasse, about 3 inches, with a lifespan of 4 to 6 years. Thirty gallons is as small as this territorial wrasse should go. The card rates them beginner, which underplays how they treat a small tank. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, pellets, and a useful habit of hunting flatworms. They are active all day and semi-aggressive about the whole footprint. A six-line eats flatworms and other pests, and it also eats ornamental shrimp. In a small tank the territorial side is the one you will see every day.",
    "Thirty gallons is enough for one 3-inch wrasse and small enough that the fish treats the whole box as a territory. It will chase new additions, harass a peaceful goby, and pick at a cleaner shrimp until the shrimp is gone. Beginner care works when the livestock plan includes that behavior: pests are the target, ornamental shrimp are not present, and the other fish are not timid cave dwellers. A six-line in a 30-gallon reef with a firefish and a sexy shrimp is three projects that cancel each other.",
    "They are active through the day and they jump. Fit a lid, because a six-line will use a feeding slot as an exit. A six-line does not need a deep sand bed the way a sand-diving Halichoeres does, but it does need rockwork to hunt through. Feed mysis and pellets so the flatworm work is not the entire diet. A tank with no pests and no feedings will push the wrasse onto whatever small invertebrate remains. Reef safe on the card means corals are generally left alone. It does not mean the reef's shrimp are safe, and it does not mean a second six-line will be tolerated.",
    "Temperature is 72 to 78°F, salinity 1.020 to 1.025, pH 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm is the reef target that matches how people usually keep them, and under 20 ppm is the fish-only allowance. Alkalinity of 8 to 12 dKH, calcium of 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium of 1250 to 1350 ppm sit with the corals. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm are assumed by the beginner label and still have to be true. Expect to pay about 15 to 30 dollars for the wrasse and about 200 to 400 for the setup. Cheap and useful against flatworms is how the fish gets added to tanks that are too small for its temper.",
    "One wrasse, a lid, meaty food, and no prized shrimp is the 30-gallon version that matches the card. The 4-to-6-year lifespan is reasonable for that layout. A six-line that spends the day pinning a goby in a corner is not being active and charming. It is being semi-aggressive in a tank with nowhere else to put the aggression. Move the wrasse, or do not combine it with the fish it is cornering. Pest control is a side effect of a predator, not a personality that stays gentle because the flatworms are gone.",
  ],

  "marine-fish/watchman-goby": [
    "The yellow watchman goby is Cryptocentrus cinctus, a small Indo-Pacific goby of about 3 to 4 inches with a lifespan of 2 to 5 years. A goby plus a pistol shrimp needs 20 gallons as the floor. Beginner care is the sand bed, the lid, and food dropped at the hole. The diet is carnivore: mysis, brine shrimp, and pellets. Toward other fish this goby stays peaceful. The goby itself does not eat coral. A yellow watchman is about 15 to 30 dollars, and the tank around it about 200 to 400. The behavior that defines the tank is the burrow. This fish needs a sand bed, and the usual partner in that burrow is a pistol shrimp. It is not a fish that hovers in the open like a firefish, and it is not a substitute for a mandarin.",
    "Pistol shrimp, often an Alpheus species, dig and maintain the tunnel. The goby watches the entrance, eats, and flicks its tail to warn the shrimp when something approaches. The shrimp has weak eyes and depends on that signal. You can keep a yellow watchman without the shrimp, and it will still dig if the sand is deep enough, but the partnership is the arrangement this animal is known for and the one that keeps the burrow occupied in the open. A dusting of sand over bare glass is not a bed. Give them a few inches of fine sand and a piece of rubble at the entrance so the tunnel does not collapse every night.",
    "They are peaceful toward fish and reef safe toward corals. The shrimp may rearrange the sand onto low frags, which is a plumbing problem, not a diet problem. Do not house the pair with sand-sifting predators or with wrasses that will eat the shrimp. A six-line or a hawkfish undoes the partnership in an evening. Twenty gallons is the minimum for one goby and a shrimp. Two watchmen that are not a pair will fight over the entrance. A known pair can share a burrow. Two strangers in 20 gallons generally will not.",
    "Water at the burrow entrance should be 72 to 82°F and 1.020 to 1.025 salinity. pH beside that entrance belongs at 8.1 to 8.4. Nitrate under 10 ppm fits a reef and under 20 ppm fits a fish-only tank, though a fish-only tank still needs the sand. Alkalinity at 8 to 12 dKH, calcium at 380 to 450 ppm, and magnesium at 1250 to 1350 ppm are the reef minerals around a burrow that may sit under live rock. Ammonia and nitrite at 0 ppm matter in a 20-gallon tank because a shrimp dump of sand can uncover a pocket of waste. Feed the goby at the entrance. Mysis and small pellets dropped by the hole reach a fish that will not cross the tank for a feeding frenzy.",
    "Beginner care is the sand, the lid, and a calm neighborhood. Watchmen can jump when they are startled off the burrow, so cover the tank. The 2-to-5-year lifespan is shorter than many marine fish on this list, which makes the first month important. A goby that never digs is often a goby with no sand, or a different species sold under a watchman label. Cryptocentrus cinctus is yellow, it stays near a hole, and it belongs with a rubble mound and a pistol shrimp more than it belongs on a bare reef floor.",
    "The card's temperament line mentions sand-sifting. What you will actually see is a shared burrow and a fish that moves sand while it maintains the entrance, not a Valenciennea that sifts the whole bed for worms. Feed it. Do not expect the shrimp's digging to replace mysis and pellets. A thin watchman next to a busy shrimp is a fish that has been watching and not eating. Drop the food at the door, keep the sand deep enough to hold a tunnel, and let the pair have 20 gallons without a predator that considers the shrimp a snack.",
  ],

};
